{ Welcome to the Catalan: one small step of the g pawn, a bishop on the long diagonal, and his whole queen side under its gaze. Open with the queen pawn. [%cal Yd2d4] }
1. d4 { Your queen pawn takes the middle and opens a road for the dark bishop. If he throws his king pawn at you with e5, take it and keep it. [%csl Gd4] } (1. e4 { The king pawn is just as strong, but it leads somewhere else entirely. } 1... e5 2. Nf3 { Level: that is the open game, where the Vienna and the Ruy Lopez live. Today the queen pawn. [%csl Be4] }) (1. Nf3 { Just as good: the knight first, and the pawns follow. } 1... d5 2. d4 Nf6 3. c4 { Level: the same house by another door, with the knight out a move early. [%csl Gf3] }) (1. c4 { Just as good: the English, where the c pawn goes first. } 1... e5 2. g3 { Level: but with his king pawn on e5 he keeps you out of the Catalan, and that is another opening. [%csl Be5] }) 1... Nf6 { He brings the king knight out. Against his queen pawn to d5 you play the very same moves, the c pawn and then the g pawn. Now the c pawn two squares, to hit the middle from the side. [%cal Yc2c4] } 2. c4 { Your c pawn hits the middle from the side: if he ever takes it, your queen or your bishop takes it back. That is the shape of the queens gambit. [%csl Gc4] } (2. Nf3 { Just as good: the knight first, and the c pawn comes next. } 2... c6 3. c4 d5 { Level: but with c6 and d5 he has built a Slav wall, and that is another opening. [%csl Bc6] }) (2. Bf4 { Just as good: the bishop out first is the London, a study of its own. } 2... d5 3. e3 { Level: a fine opening, just not today. [%csl Bf4] }) (2. g3 { Just as good: the bishop goes to the long diagonal first. [%cal Gf1g2] } 2... d5 3. Nf3 c5 { Level: but his c pawn hits d4 before your own c pawn has moved, and the fight is a different one. [%cal Rc5d4] }) (2. e3 { Just as good, if quieter: the e pawn shuts your dark bishop in behind it. } 2... e6 3. Nf3 d5 { Level: solid, but the bishop on c1 has no road out and you have no pressure anywhere. [%csl Rc1] }) 2... e6 { He frees his dark bishop with the e pawn. His g pawn to g6 would be the kings Indian, and his c pawn to c5 the Benoni, your other study. Now the Catalan move: the g pawn one step. [%cal Yg2g3] } 3. g3 { That is the Catalan: the g pawn makes room, and your bishop will stand on g2 and look along the long diagonal at his queen side. [%cal Gf1g2,Gg2b7] } (3. Nf3 { Just as good: the knight first, and the g pawn next. } 3... d5 4. g3 { Level: the Catalan by another door, one move later. [%csl Gg3] }) (3. Nc3 { A fine move, but it invites the pin on b4, and that is the Nimzo Indian, a whole different opening. } 3... Bb4 { Level: his bishop pins the knight to your king, and your plans change completely. [%cal Rb4c3] }) (3. Bf4 { Just as good: the London bishop, though with the c pawn already out it has less to do here. } 3... d5 { Level: he takes the middle, and your bishop on f4 bites on nothing. [%csl Bf4] }) (3. e3 { Just as good, if quieter: the e pawn shuts the dark bishop in. } 3... Be7 4. Nf3 d5 { Level: solid and slow, and your bishop on c1 stays at home. [%csl Rc1] }) 3... d5 { He takes the middle with his d pawn. Bishop to g2, onto the long diagonal. [%cal Yf1g2,Gg2b7] } 4. Bg2 { Your bishop sits on the long diagonal and presses on his d pawn, with his queen side behind it. A check on b4 instead is met by your bishop to d2, and his bishop drops back. [%cal Gg2d5] } (4. Nf3 { Just as good: the knight first, the bishop next. } 4... Bb4+ 5. Bd2 Be7 { Level: his check came to nothing, and the bishop goes to g2 next. [%csl Gf3] }) (4. Nc3 { The knight blocks the c column, and his c pawn hits back at once: that is a different opening. } 4... c5 { Level: his c pawn strikes at d4 while your bishop still sits at home. [%cal Rc5d4] }) (4. cxd5 { Trading on d5 opens the road for his light bishop and takes the pressure off. } 4... exd5 { Level: your bishop, once it reaches g2, would have only his pawn on d5 to stare at. [%csl Rd5] }) (4. e3 { The pawn guards d4, but after his capture your bishop takes back on c4 instead of reaching the long diagonal. [%cal Ge3d4] } 4... dxc4 5. Bxc4 { Level: that is a different opening, and his queen side pawns will chase the bishop. [%csl Bc4] }) 4... Be7 { He develops the bishop quietly. His c pawn two squares instead would be the Tarrasch after you take on d5, another opening. Knight to f3. [%cal Yg1f3] } 5. Nf3 { The king knight comes out and guards d4, and your king can castle next. [%cal Gf3d4] } (5. Nc3 { Also fine, but the king knight comes first, and this one finds its square later. } 5... O-O 6. Nf3 dxc4 { Level: he takes the pawn, and with your knight on c3 it is harder to win back. [%csl Rc4] }) (5. Qc2 { The queen first is fine too; the knight simply comes next. } 5... O-O 6. Nf3 { Level: the same pieces, in another order. [%csl Gc2] }) (5. Bf4 { The bishop out early is playable, but your knight belongs on f3 before the bishop chooses a square. } 5... O-O 6. Nf3 { Level: nothing wrong, and nothing gained. [%csl Bf4] }) (5. Qb3 { Just as good: the queen hits b7, and his c pawn strikes back at once to open the middle. } 5... c5 { Level: his c pawn hits d4, and your queen stands in the way of your own b pawn. [%cal Rc5d4] }) 5... O-O { He castles. Castle too: in the Catalan your king sits safe behind the bishop while the fight starts on the other wing. [%cal Ye1g1] } 6. O-O { Your king is safe behind the bishop. If he keeps his d pawn where it stands, with a knight move or a b pawn step, that is the closed Catalan: your queen goes to c2 just the same. [%csl Gg1] } (6. Nc3 { Fine too, but castling first keeps your choices open, and this knight can wait for its best square. } 6... dxc4 7. Ne5 { Level: he has taken the pawn, and your knight has to go and get it back. [%csl Rc4] }) (6. b3 { Just as good: the b pawn guards c4 early, and his c pawn hits back. [%cal Gb3c4] } 6... c5 { Level: his c pawn strikes at d4 while your king is still at home. [%cal Rc5d4] }) (6. Qc2 { Just as good: the queen before castling, though it lets him check on a5. } 6... c5 7. dxc5 Qa5+ { Level: his queen comes out with check, and you must block it before you can castle. [%cal Ra5e1] }) (6. Qd3 { Just as good: the queen can stand on d3 too, though his pieces can hit her there. } 6... b6 { Level: his bishop will come to a6 and look straight at her. [%csl Rd3] }) 6... dxc4 { He takes the pawn on c4: that is the main line. Bring the queen to c2: she eyes that pawn and his h pawn in front of the king. [%cal Yd1c2,Gc2h7] } 7. Qc2 { The queen hits the pawn on c4 and eyes h7 in front of his king: he cannot keep the pawn for long. [%cal Gc2c4,Gc2h7] } (7. Ne5 { Just as good: the other main line, where the knight jumps in at once and hits c4. } 7... Nc6 8. Bxc6 bxc6 9. Nxc6 Qe8 10. Nxe7+ Qxe7 { Level: the pieces came off in pairs and the pawns are even; a fine Catalan, only a different one. [%csl Gc6] }) (7. Qa4 { Just as good: from a4 she also takes on c4. } 7... a6 8. Qxc4 b5 9. Qc2 { Level: the very same position as the main line, by another square. [%csl Gc2] }) (7. a4 { Just as good: the a pawn stops b5 for ever, and his knight hits d4 at once. } 7... Nc6 8. Na3 { Level: your knight has to go to the rim to get the pawn back, and his pieces are out just as fast as yours. [%csl Ra3] }) (7. Na3 { Just as good: the knight goes for c4, though his bishop can take it first and double your a pawns. } 7... Bxa3 8. bxa3 { Level: you will get the pawn back, but the a pawns are ugly for the rest of the game. [%csl Ra3] }) (7. Nc3 { The knight on c3 does nothing against his extra pawn, and his knight comes out to hit d4 at once. } 7... Nc6 8. e4 { Level: his knight hits d4, his pawn on c4 still lives, and this is a harder road for you. [%csl Rc4] }) 7... a6 { He guards b5 with the a pawn, so his extra pawn could be held with b5 next. Take it now. [%cal Ra6b5,Yc2c4] } 8. Qxc4 { The queen takes the pawn back, and the pawn count is level again. [%csl Gc4] } (8. a4 { Just as good, and the modern way: the a pawn stops b5, and the pawn comes back a move later. } 8... Bd7 9. Qxc4 Bc6 { Level: his bishop on c6 contests the long diagonal, and both sides are fine. [%csl Ga4] }) (8. Rd1 { Just as good: the rook first, though he can hold the pawn with b5 and you need b3 to crack it open. } 8... b5 9. Ne5 { Level: his pawn on c4 is still his, and winning it back costs moves. [%csl Rc4] }) (8. Bg5 { The bishop pins nothing yet, and b5 keeps his pawn: take it while you can. } 8... b5 { About level: his queen side pawns hold the extra pawn, and your queen has to find another way in. [%csl Rc4] }) (8. Nc3 { The knight on c3 lets his knight hit d4, and his extra pawn stays his. } 8... Nc6 { About level: his pieces come out with threats while you are still a pawn down. [%cal Rc6d4] }) (8. Bf4 { The bishop comes out, but b5 keeps the pawn and your b pawn has to go and get it. } 8... b5 9. b3 { About level: a pawn down and chasing it, with his pieces free. [%csl Rc4] }) 8... b5 { His b pawn kicks the queen and gains space. Back to c2: she keeps the c column and his h pawn in view. [%cal Yc4c2,Gc2h7] } 9. Qc2 { The queen steps back. She still watches his h pawn and the whole c column, and his advanced queen side pawns are targets now. [%csl Ra6,Rb5][%cal Gc2h7] } (9. Qd3 { The queen is fine here too, but on c2 she keeps the c column and his king side in view. } 9... Bb7 { Level: nothing lost, but the queen stands in the way of your rooks on the d column. [%csl Bd3] }) (9. Qc3 { Just as good: on c3 the queen stands on the other long diagonal, though his bishop and knight can hit her there. [%csl Rc3] } 9... Bb7 10. Bf4 Nd5 { Level: his knight kicks the queen and the bishop at once, and she must move again. [%cal Rd5c3] }) (9. Qb3 { Just as good: the queen on b3, though she blocks your own b pawn and his bishop can hit her from d5. } 9... Bb7 { Level: she will have to move again when the bishop lands on d5. [%csl Bb3] }) (9. Ne5 { Look again: his b pawn hits your queen, and she is still on c4. [%cal Rb5c4] }) (9. Qc5 { Look again: his bishop on e7 takes your queen. [%cal Re7c5] }) 9... Bb7 { His bishop takes the long diagonal too, facing yours with your knight between them. Bring your dark bishop out to d2; from there it can reach a5. [%cal Rb7f3,Yc1d2,Gd2a5] } 10. Bd2 { Your dark bishop steps out and eyes a5, the square from which it sits on his c pawn. If his rook climbs to a7 instead of what comes next, bring a rook to the c column. [%cal Gd2a5] } (10. Bf4 { Just as good: the bishop eyes his c pawn at once. Going by d2 first keeps a5 in reserve if his queen knight comes out. } 10... Nc6 11. Nc3 { Level: a fine Catalan too, with the bishop on the other diagonal. [%csl Gf4] }) (10. Bg5 { Just as good, and a simpler plan: trade this bishop for his knight and bring your knight to b3 and c5. } 10... Nbd7 11. Bxf6 Nxf6 12. Nbd2 { Level: your knight heads for b3, and his bishop pair is his only trump. [%cal Gd2b3] }) (10. a4 { Just as good: the a pawn pokes at b5, though his pawns hold and your bishop is still at home. } 10... Nbd7 { Level: his pawns hold, and the dark bishop still waits on c1. [%csl Rc1] }) (10. Rd1 { The rook first is fine, but the dark bishop must come out before the rooks know where they belong. } 10... Nbd7 { Level: a move spent, and the bishop on c1 still blocks the rook on a1. [%csl Rc1] }) (10. Nc3 { Just as good: the knight on c3, though it is a target for his b pawn and blocks your queen on the c column. } 10... Nbd7 11. Be3 { Level: his b pawn can kick the knight whenever he likes. [%cal Rb5b4] }) (10. Be3 { Just as good: the bishop on e3, though it blocks your own e pawn and has less to aim at. } 10... Nbd7 { Level: nothing wrong, but from d2 the bishop can reach a5 and sit on his c pawn. [%csl Bd2] }) 10... Be4 { His bishop hits your queen from e4. Step her back to c1, where she still guards your dark bishop and keeps the c column. [%cal Yc2c1] } 11. Qc1 { The queen steps back and keeps the c column; his bishop gained nothing. A knight to d7 meets your bishop pinning his c pawn, a later chapter, and his queen to c8 is just slow. [%csl Gc1] } (11. Qd1 { Just as good: the queen goes home, though his c pawn then breaks free at once. } 11... c5 { Level: his c pawn hits d4, and the fight for the c column is lost before it began. [%cal Rc7c5] }) (11. Qc3 { On c3 she is kicked by his b pawn and has to run twice. } 11... b4 12. Qb3 { About level: two queen moves for nothing, and his pawns roll forward. [%cal Rb5b4] }) (11. Qb3 { From b3 she watches b5, but his c pawn breaks free and his pieces come out with gain of time. [%cal Gb3b5] } 11... c5 { About level: the c pawn hits d4, and she is in the way of your own b pawn. [%cal Rc7c5] }) (11. Qxe4 { Look again: his knight on f6 takes your queen. [%cal Rf6e4] }) (11. Rc1 { Look again: his bishop takes your queen on c2. [%cal Re4c2] }) 11... Bb7 { His bishop goes back where it came from: two moves for nothing. A pawn or a knight to c6 instead, and you simply keep developing. Now the bishop to f4. [%cal Yd2f4] } 12. Bf4 { Your bishop sits on f4 and eyes his c pawn. If his bishop comes to d6 to swap it off, let him take: your queen takes back, not the g pawn. [%cal Gf4c7] } (12. a3 { Just as good: the a pawn makes room for b4, and the bishop goes to a5 to pin his c pawn down. } 12... Nbd7 13. Ba5 { Level: the bishop sits on a5 and his c pawn cannot move. [%csl Ga5] }) (12. Rd1 { Just as good: the rook first, and the bishop still wants f4. } 12... Qc8 13. Bf4 { Level: the rook and the bishop simply swapped turns. [%csl Gd1] }) (12. Qc2 { Just as good: back to c2, and his bishop can hit her again if he likes. } 12... Ra7 { Level: his rook creeps to a7 to guard the bishop, and the fight goes on. [%csl Gc2] }) (12. a4 { Just as good: the a pawn hits b5, and the rooks come off on the a column. } 12... h6 13. Bf4 { Level: the a column will open and empty, and that helps nobody. [%csl Ba4] }) (12. b4 { Just as good: the b pawn fixes his pawns, and his knight jumps to e4 to eye your bishop. } 12... Ne4 { Level: his knight sits in the middle and eyes your bishop, and the b pawn push gained nothing. [%cal Re4d2] }) 12... Nd5 { His knight jumps to d5 and hits your bishop. Bring your knight to c3: it hits the knight back and guards the middle. [%cal Yb1c3] } 13. Nc3 { Your knight hits his knight on d5 and guards e4. If he takes the bishop, you take back with the queen. [%cal Gc3d5,Gc3e4] } (13. Rd1 { Just as good: the rook first, and his c pawn breaks free, but you are ready for it. } 13... c5 { Level: a fine game, with his knight still sitting on d5. [%csl Gd1] }) (13. Bg5 { Just as good: the bishop runs to g5, and a trade on e7 comes later. } 13... Nd7 { Level: the bishop has moved three times, and his pieces come out. [%csl Bg5] }) (13. a4 { Just as good: the a pawn pokes at b5, though his knight still hits your bishop. } 13... Nd7 { Level: he has time for everything while your bishop on f4 still has his knight staring at it. [%csl Rf4] }) (13. Ne5 { Just as good: the knight jumps in, and his c pawn breaks free while your bishop stays under his knight's eye. } 13... c5 { Level: his pawns roll, and your bishop on f4 is still under his knight's eye. [%cal Rc7c5] }) (13. Nbd2 { This knight blocks your queen's guard of f4, so the pawn has to take back and your king's cover opens. } 13... Nxf4 14. gxf4 { Level: your king side pawns are broken, and the queen stays home. [%csl Rf4] }) 13... Nxf4 { He takes your bishop. Take back with the queen, not the pawn: she lands on f4 eyeing his c pawn, and your king keeps its pawn cover. [%cal Yc1f4] } 14. Qxf4 { Your queen eyes his backward c pawn on the half open c column, your knight eyes b5, and your bishop waits on the long diagonal behind its knight. That is the Catalan: nothing won yet, and everything of his under pressure. [%csl Rc7][%cal Gf4c7,Gc3b5] } (14. gxf4 { Playable too, but it splits the pawns in front of your king, and the queen stays at home. } 14... Nd7 15. Rd1 { Level: nothing lost, but your king side pawns are split and the queen has no good square. [%csl Rf4] }) 1-0


[Event "Catalan: he holds the pawn with b5"]
[Result "1-0"]
