{ The main line, one move before he usually gives the pawn back: your queen on c2 stares at his pawn on c4, and he has to decide whether to hold on to it. [%cal Gc2c4] }
7... b5 { He holds the pawn with his b pawn instead of the quiet a6: greedy, and it leaves his queen side loose. What now? [%csl Rc4] } 8. a4 { Your a pawn hits the prop on b5. If his bishop comes to b7 instead, take the pawn and keep taking, and the pawn comes back with interest. [%cal Ga4b5] } (8. Ne5 { The knight jumps in, but his c pawn simply props up the b pawn and he keeps everything. } 8... c6 9. Rd1 Nd5 { About level: his two pawns prop each other up, and your knight on e5 has nothing to bite. [%csl Rc4] }) (8. Nc3 { The knight comes out, but it does nothing against the two pawns, and his c pawn backs them up. } 8... c6 { About level: he keeps the extra pawn for now, and your pieces have no way in. [%csl Rc4] }) (8. Nfd2 { The knight heads for c4, but his knight blocks the bishop's diagonal first, and his c pawn comes up to prop b5. } 8... Nd5 9. a4 c6 { About level: the right idea a move late: his pawn chain stands, and you still have work to do. [%csl Rc4] }) (8. b3 { The b pawn hits c4, but he simply takes it, and after your queen takes back he is still a pawn up. } 8... cxb3 9. Qxb3 a6 { About level: the pawn count has not changed, and his pawns on a6 and b5 hold together. [%cal Rb5b4] }) (8. Bg5 { The bishop looks active, but it does nothing about the pawns, and he quietly tidies up. } 8... a6 9. Ne5 Ra7 { About level: his rook guards everything from a7, and the extra pawn is still his. [%csl Rc4] }) (8. Qxc4 { Look again: your queen walks straight into his b pawn. [%cal Rb5c4] }) (8. e4 { The e pawn takes the middle, but it does nothing about his pawns on the queen side. } 8... Nc6 { Slightly better for him: his knight hits d4, and his b pawn still holds c4. [%csl Rc4] }) (8. Bf4 { The bishop comes out, but it is the a pawn that cracks his pawn chain, and that move has not been played. } 8... Bb7 { About level: his bishop comes out, and the extra pawn stays his for now. [%csl Rc4] }) (8. Nbd2 { The knight heads for c4, but his b pawn guards that square, and he simply finishes developing. } 8... Bb7 { Slightly better for him: the pawn chain holds, and your knight has no way through. [%csl Rc4] }) (8. Rd1 { A rook move that waits, and he simply finishes developing. } 8... Bb7 { Slightly better for him: he keeps the pawn and finishes developing while you shuffle. [%csl Rc4] }) (8. d5 { The pawn push opens the middle, but it simply loses a pawn. } 8... exd5 { Better for him: your d pawn is gone, and his pawn on c4 is still standing. [%csl Rd5] }) 8... b4 { He pushes past and hangs on to the pawn. His pawn to c6 instead, to prop everything up, is a mistake: a puzzle at the end of this study. How do you get at his pawn now? [%csl Rc4] } 9. Nfd2 { Your knight steps back, and two things happen at once: it hits c4, and your bishop now sees all the way along the diagonal to his rook on a8. [%cal Gd2c4,Gg2a8] } (9. Nbd2 { Just as good: the other knight heads for c4, and the pawn falls all the same. } 9... Bb7 10. Nxc4 c5 { Level: the pawn is back, and both of you have a free hand. [%csl Gc4] }) (9. Ne5 { Just as good: the knight jumps in and hits c4, and his queen grabs your d pawn in return. } 9... Qxd4 10. Nxc4 Nd5 { Level: you got the c pawn, he got your d pawn, and his queen sits in the middle of the board. [%csl Rd4] }) (9. Bg5 { The bishop does not help: his bishop comes to a6 and guards the pawn from behind. } 9... Ba6 10. Nbd2 b3 11. Qd1 Nc6 { Level: his pawns keep coming, your queen had to run home, and his pieces are all out. [%cal Rb4b3] }) (9. Qxc4 { The queen grabs the pawn, but his bishop hits her from a6 and he gains time for his pieces. } 9... Ba6 10. Qc2 Nbd7 { About level: the pawn is back, but his pieces came out with gain of time and your queen has moved three times. [%cal Ra6c4] }) (9. Nc3 { Look again: the knight lands where his b pawn can take it. [%cal Rb4c3] }) (9. Bf4 { The bishop comes out, but his knight jumps to d5 and hits it, and the pawns are still his. } 9... Nd5 10. Ne5 Ba6 { Level: his bishop guards c4 from a6, and your pieces are chasing shadows. [%csl Rc4] }) (9. Be3 { The bishop comes out, and his knight simply jumps in and trades it. } 9... Nd5 { Level: the bishop will be swapped off, and your knight still has to go and fetch the pawn. [%csl Rc4] }) (9. Rd1 { The rook first lets his bishop come to a6, where it guards the pawn. } 9... Ba6 { About level: the pawn on c4 has a guard now, and your rook was in no hurry. [%cal Ra6c4] }) (9. e3 { The e pawn props up d4, but the pawn he took is the problem, and this does nothing about it. } 9... Ba6 { Slightly better for him: the bishop on a6 holds his extra pawn, and your pieces are stuck behind their own pawns. [%cal Ra6c4] }) (9. Bd2 { The bishop steps out, but his bishop answers from a6 and the pawn stays his. } 9... Ba6 { Slightly better for him: the pawn on c4 has its guard, and your bishop does nothing from d2. [%cal Ra6c4] }) 9... Nd5 { His knight jumps to d5 and blocks the diagonal, so the rook is safe. A pawn to c6 would block it too, and changes nothing. What now? [%csl Rb4] } 10. Nxc4 { The pawn is back. His b pawn has no pawn left to guard it, and your knight on c4 eyes his queen side. [%csl Gc4,Rb4] } (10. e4 { The pawn kicks his knight, but his b pawn runs to b3 and your queen has to deal with it first. } 10... b3 11. Qxc4 Ba6 { Level: you got the pawn back, but his bishop hits your queen, and his knight slips away next. [%cal Ra6c4] }) (10. Re1 { The rook steps aside, but his c pawn breaks free and the pawn on c4 is still his. } 10... c5 11. dxc5 Ba6 { About level: his bishop guards c4 from behind and his pieces are out; a slow road. [%csl Rc4] }) (10. Qxc4 { The queen takes, but his bishop hits her from a6 at once, and the knight was ready to do the job. } 10... Ba6 11. Qc2 c5 { Better for him: his pieces came out hitting your queen, and his c pawn breaks free with gain of time. [%cal Ra6c4] }) (10. Nc3 { Look again: the knight walks into his b pawn. [%cal Rb4c3] }) (10. Bxd5 { The Catalan bishop takes the knight, and that is the one piece you never trade away. } 10... exd5 { Much better for him: you gave up the long diagonal for a knight, and his pawn on d5 now guards c4 for good. [%csl Rd5] }) (10. a5 { The a pawn runs on, but the pawn on c4 was the point, and it is still his. } 10... c5 { About level: his c pawn joins the fight and the pawn chain holds for now. [%csl Rc4] }) (10. Rd1 { The rook waits, and his b pawn runs to b3 to hit your queen. } 10... b3 { Slightly better for him: his pawns roll forward with gain of time, and the knight never took on c4. [%cal Rb4b3] }) (10. e3 { A quiet pawn move, when the knight was ready to take on c4. } 10... c5 { Better for him: his c pawn breaks in the middle, and the pawn you wanted back is still there. [%csl Rc4] }) (10. Nf3 { The knight goes back where it came from, two moves for nothing. } 10... Ba6 { Better for him: his bishop guards the pawn from a6, and your knight has wandered. [%cal Ra6c4] }) 10... c5 { His c pawn steps up to hit your d pawn, guarded by the bishop behind it. A quiet bishop or knight move instead would let your knight hop to a5 and on into c6, and the a pawn push just loses that pawn to your knights. What now? } 11. dxc5 { Your d pawn takes, and he must spend a move taking back with the bishop. [%csl Gc5] } (11. e4 { Just as good: the e pawn kicks the knight first, and his knight goes to b6 to hit yours on c4. } 11... Nb6 12. dxc5 Nxc4 { Level: your queen takes the knight back, and the pawn on c5 is his to collect. [%csl Rc4] }) (11. Ne3 { The knight hits his knight, but he takes on d4 and the trade on d5 opens his bishop's road. } 11... cxd4 12. Nxd5 exd5 { About level: a pawn down for the moment, with his pawn on d5 shutting your bishop until you win it back. [%csl Rd5] }) (11. Rd1 { The rook comes to the d column, but he takes on d4 and the rook has to take back. } 11... cxd4 12. Rxd4 Nc6 { About level: his knight hits your rook, and the bishop comes to a6 next to hit your knight. [%cal Rc6d4] }) (11. Nbd2 { The second knight comes out, but he takes on d4 and keeps the pawn. } 11... cxd4 12. Ne5 Bb7 { About level: a pawn down for the moment, and his bishop on b7 guards everything along the diagonal. [%csl Rd4] }) 11... Bxc5 { His bishop takes the pawn back, and from c5 it guards his b pawn too; a bishop to a6 instead, hitting your knight, would change nothing. What now? [%csl Rc5] } 12. e4 { The e pawn kicks his knight and takes the middle. The pawn is back, his knight must run, and once it goes his pawn on b4 has only the bishop to hold it. [%cal Ge4d5][%csl Rb4] } (12. Rd1 { The rook comes to the d column first; his knight settles on d7, and the pawn kick comes a move later. } 12... Nd7 { About level: a fine game, but the pawn kick first was the sharper order. [%csl Bd1] }) (12. Ne3 { Just as good: the knight hits his knight, and his queen steps to c7 to guard his bishop; the pawn kick is simply more direct. } 12... Qc7 { Level: his queen on c7 guards the bishop, and the game goes on with chances for both. [%csl Bc7] }) (12. Be3 { The bishop offers a trade, and his bishop steps back to e7. } 12... Be7 { Level: nothing wrong, but the e pawn was the move that kicks his knight. [%csl Be3] }) (12. Nbd2 { The second knight comes out, but his bishop comes to a6 and hits your knight on c4. } 12... Ba6 { Level: the knight on c4 is hit and goes to b3, and nothing was gained. [%cal Ra6c4] }) (12. Ne5 { The knight jumps in, but his queen comes to c7 and hits it, and his knight on d5 stays put. } 12... Qc7 { Level: the knight has to justify itself on e5, and the pawn kick was the stronger move. [%cal Rc7e5] }) (12. b3 { The b pawn props up your knight, but his knight comes to d7 and his bishop to a6, and the pawn kick never comes. } 12... Nd7 { Level: a quiet game; the e pawn push was the move that kicked his knight. [%csl Bb3] }) 1-0


