{ The main line, with your dark bishop just out to d2. His queen side pawns have stepped forward, and his bishop faces yours past your knight. [%csl Gd2][%cal Rb7f3] }
10... Nbd7 { He brings the queen knight out to d7, a natural square, and his queen side looks settled. What now? } 11. Ba5 { The bishop lands on the far side and pins his c pawn to his queen: while she stays at home, that pawn cannot move. A knight to b6 or a bishop to d6 changes nothing. [%cal Ga5d8] } (11. b4 { The b pawn fixes his queen side, but his bishop kicks your queen first and his knight heads for c4. } 11... Be4 12. Qc1 Nb6 { Level: the bishop never reached a5, and his knight is on its way to c4. [%cal Gb6c4] }) (11. Rd1 { The rook comes to the middle, but his c pawn breaks free at once, before your bishop can pin it. } 11... c5 12. dxc5 Bxc5 { About level: his c pawn is gone and so is your grip; his pieces are free. [%csl Rc5] }) (11. Bg5 { The bishop goes the other way, but on g5 it pins nothing that matters, and his c pawn breaks free. } 11... Rc8 12. a4 c5 { About level: he got his c pawn moving, and your grip on his queen side never happened. [%csl Rc5] }) (11. a4 { The a pawn pokes his pawns, but his b pawn steps past and your bishop is still looking for a job. } 11... b4 12. Be3 Nd5 { About level: his pawn on b4 cramps your queen side instead of the other way round. [%csl Rb4] }) (11. Nc3 { The knight comes out, but it walks into his b pawn and the c pawn breaks free. } 11... Rc8 12. Rac1 c5 { About level: his c pawn is free, and your knight on c3 is a target for his pawns. [%cal Rb5b4] }) (11. Ne5 { The knight jumps in, but it lets his bishop trade off your Catalan bishop, and a queen check follows. } 11... Bxg2 12. Kxg2 Nxe5 13. dxe5 Qd5+ { Better for him: the queen check picks up your pawn on e5, and your best piece is gone. [%cal Rd5e5] }) (11. Rc1 { The rook comes to the c column, but it does nothing to stop his c pawn from moving. } 11... c5 { About level: his c pawn broke free at once, before your bishop could pin it. [%cal Rc7c5] }) (11. Be3 { The bishop takes a natural square, but his knight jumps to d5 and hits it, and his c pawn gets ready to move. } 11... Nd5 { About level: the dark bishops will come off, and his c pawn is free to go. [%cal Rd5e3] }) (11. Bf4 { On f4 the bishop eyes c7, but his c pawn simply steps forward. } 11... c5 { Slightly better for him: the pawn on c5 is past your bishop, and his pieces come alive. [%cal Rc7c5] }) (11. e3 { A slow pawn move, and his a pawn starts running. } 11... a5 { Slightly better for him: his queen side pawns gain space, and your bishop never reached a5. [%cal Ra6a5] }) (11. e4 { The e pawn strikes, but it is simply taken by his bishop. } 11... Bxe4 { Better for him: a free pawn, and his bishop sits in the middle of the board. [%csl Re4] }) 11... Rc8 { He puts his rook behind the c pawn, which your bishop and queen both hit. Nothing of yours hangs: what is your most useful move? [%csl Rc8] } 12. Nbd2 { Your last piece comes out and eyes the middle. A bishop to d6 or a knight back to b8 from him changes nothing. [%cal Gd2b3,Gd2e4] } (12. Rc1 { Just as good: the rook takes the c column first, and the knight comes next. } 12... Nb6 13. Nbd2 c5 { Level: his knight blocked the bishop's diagonal, so the c pawn moves at last, but your rook is ready for it. [%csl Gc1] }) (12. Rd1 { The rook on d1 is fine, but it has less to do there than on the c column. } 12... Qe8 { Level: nothing wrong with it, and the fight goes on. [%csl Bd1] }) (12. a4 { Just as good: the a pawn pokes at b5, and his b pawn steps past to gain space. } 12... b4 13. Nbd2 Qe8 14. Qd3 c5 { Level: his pawn on b4 cramps you, and his c pawn broke free in the end. [%csl Rb4] }) (12. e3 { Too quiet: it guards d4, but the knight on b1 still waits at home, and his c pawn gets ready to move. [%cal Ge3d4] } 12... Nb6 13. Rd1 c5 { Level: his knight blocked the bishop's diagonal, and the c pawn is free. [%csl Rc5] }) (12. Ne5 { The knight jumps in, but it lets him trade off your Catalan bishop before his queen checks. } 12... Bxg2 13. Kxg2 Nxe5 14. dxe5 Qd5+ { Better for him: your bishop is gone, and the queen check wins the pawn on e5. [%cal Rd5e5] }) (12. e4 { The pawn steps straight into his bishop's diagonal. } 12... Bxe4 { Better for him: he simply took it, a free pawn. [%csl Re4] }) (12. a3 { A small pawn move that prepares b4, but his knight jumps to b6 first. } 12... Nb6 { Level: his knight lands on b6, in front of your bishop, and the plan on c7 has to wait. [%csl Rb6] }) (12. b3 { The b pawn step is too slow: his knight goes back to b8 and the c pawn gets ready. } 12... Nb8 { Level: he regroups, and once his queen steps off the diagonal his c pawn is free to move. [%csl Rb8] }) (12. Qc1 { The queen steps back to c1, but his queen answers from e8. } 12... Qe8 { Level: his queen guards everything from e8, and your knight is still at home. [%csl Re8] }) (12. Nc3 { The knight on c3 walks into his b pawn's path and blocks your own queen. } 12... Nb8 { Better for him: his knight heads for c6 to hit your bishop and d4, and his b pawn can kick your knight. [%cal Rb5b4] }) (12. Bxc7 { The bishop grabs the c pawn, and never comes out again. } 12... Rxc7 { Lost for you: his rook simply takes the bishop. [%cal Rc8c7] }) (12. Qd3 { The queen steps up to d3, but your knight on b1 still waits at home, and his queen steps off the diagonal. } 12... Qe8 { Level: his c pawn is free to move again, and your queen knight is still at home. [%csl Rb1] }) 12... Qe8 { His queen steps off the diagonal, so his c pawn is no longer pinned; a knight to b6 was his better try, and a bishop to d6 runs into your e pawn. What now? [%csl Re8] } 13. b4 { The b pawn locks the queen side: his a and b pawns are stuck, your bishop is guarded, and his c pawn cannot step up, as your b and d pawns both hit c5. [%csl Ra6,Rb5][%cal Gb4c5,Gd4c5] } (13. a4 { The a pawn hits b5, but his c pawn breaks out and the pawns come off. } 13... c5 14. axb5 axb5 15. dxc5 Nxc5 { Level: the queen side has opened up, and your grip on it is gone. [%csl Rc5] }) (13. Rac1 { The rook takes the c column, but with his queen off the diagonal his c pawn breaks free anyway. } 13... c5 14. Qb1 cxd4 { Level: the middle has opened, and your bishop on a5 has nothing left to nail. [%csl Rd4] }) (13. Rfc1 { The other rook to the c column: the same story, his c pawn breaks out. } 13... c5 14. Qd1 c4 { Level: his pawns rolled forward, and your grip on his queen side is gone. [%csl Rc4] }) (13. e4 { The pawn takes the middle, but his c pawn breaks free now that his queen is out of the pin. } 13... c5 14. Qd3 cxd4 { About level: the middle opened, and the bishop on a5 lost its job. [%csl Rd4] }) (13. Ne5 { The knight jumps in, but it lets him trade off your Catalan bishop. } 13... Bxg2 14. Kxg2 c5 { Better for him: your Catalan bishop is gone, and his c pawn is free. [%csl Rc5] }) (13. Bc3 { The bishop steps back, but it walks into his b pawn with every retreat blocked by your own men. } 13... b4 14. Bxb4 Bxb4 { Much better for him: a whole bishop for a pawn. [%csl Rb4] }) (13. Qd1 { The queen steps home, and his c pawn breaks free at once. } 13... c5 { Level: his c pawn is free, and his pieces have room. [%cal Rc7c5] }) (13. Rad1 { A rook to the d column, but the moment was for the b pawn: his c pawn moves. } 13... c5 { Level: the pawn on c5 undoes the whole idea of the bishop on a5. [%cal Rc7c5] }) (13. Nb3 { The knight backs up the bishop, but his c pawn still breaks. } 13... c5 { Level: the queen side opens, and the grip is a memory. [%cal Rc7c5] }) (13. Bxc7 { The bishop grabs the c pawn, and his bishop traps it there. } 13... Bd6 { Much better for him: the bishop on c7 cannot get out, and his rook collects it. [%cal Rd6c7] }) 1-0


