 level: the pawn comes back later, but he got his queen side going first. [%csl Rc4] }) (7. Bd2 { Just as good: the bishop comes out early, and he takes on c4. } 7... dxc4 { About level: a pawn to win back, and the bishop on d2 is not helping. [%csl Rc4] }) (7. e3 { The e pawn props up d4 but shuts in your bishop, and he takes on c4. } 7... dxc4 { About level: his pawn on c4 is a nuisance, and your dark bishop is behind a wall. [%csl Rc4] }) (7. Qa4 { The queen goes to a4, where his b pawn kicks her at once. } 7... b5 { About level: his b pawn came forward with gain of time, and the queen is on the wrong side. [%cal Rb7b5] }) (7. Qd3 { The queen guards c4 from d3 too, and she eyes his king side from there. [%cal Gd3c4] } 7... b6 { Just as good: she is a shade more exposed on d3, but the game is the same. [%csl Bd3] }) (7. Bf4 { Just as good: the bishop comes out before the queen; he takes on c4, and his knight hops to d5 to trade it off. } 7... dxc4 { Level: your knight wins the pawn back through e5, but his knight lands on d5 and trades your bishop. [%csl Rc4] }) (7. Na3 { Just as good: the knight on the rim means to take on c4 later, while his pieces come out. } 7... Nbd7 { Level: a quiet game where your knight has to come back in from the edge. [%csl Ba3] }) (7. Bg5 { Just as good: the bishop looks active; he takes on c4 and holds it with b5 for a while. } 7... dxc4 { Level: the pawn is won back on the queen side after some fireworks, and your bishop on g5 played no part. [%csl Rc4] }) (7. a4 { Just as good: the a pawn takes space on the wing, and the queen comes out a move later. } 7... a5 { Level: both a pawns have moved, and your queen still has to find her square. [%csl Ba4] }) 7... Nbd7 { He brings the queen knight to d7, the usual square, and keeps everything guarded. Nothing is hanging on either side: what does your position still need? [%csl Rd7] } 8. Nbd2 { Your queen knight comes out to d2, where it guards the middle and keeps c4 covered. [%csl Gd2] } (8. Rd1 { Just as good: the rook first, and the knight comes next. } 8... b6 9. Nbd2 Ba6 10. e4 Nxe4 { Level: the same plan, and the pawn strike came anyway with his bishop on a6. [%cal Ra6c4] }) (8. b3 { Also fine: the b pawn guards c4 and makes room for the bishop. [%cal Gb3c4] } 8... b6 9. Rd1 h6 { Level: a slower set-up, and his bishop will come to b7 or a6. [%cal Gc1b2] }) (8. Bf4 { Just as good: the bishop comes out, though it has little to bite on and the knight on b1 is still at home. } 8... b6 { Level: his bishop goes to b7 next, and your bishop on f4 stares at nothing. [%csl Bf4] }) (8. e4 { The pawn strikes before the knight backs it up, and his knight takes it. } 8... Nxe4 { Slightly better for him: a free pawn, and nothing to show for it. [%csl Re4] }) (8. Ne5 { The knight jumps in, but he trades it and your pawn lands on e5 too soon. } 8... Nxe5 9. dxe5 Ng4 { Slightly better for him: your pawn on e5 is a target now, and his knight on g4 is a nuisance. [%cal Rg4e5] }) (8. Nc3 { The knight on c3 lets him take on c4, and that pawn is hard to win back. } 8... dxc4 9. a4 a5 { Level: the pawn on c4 takes work to win back, and his queen side is solid. [%csl Rc4] }) (8. cxd5 { The trade on d5 lets his pawn out of its box and frees his bishop. } 8... exd5 { About level: his e pawn took back, his bishop on c8 is free, and the long diagonal hits a wall. [%csl Rd5] }) (8. Qb3 { The queen slides to b3, where his a pawn and b pawn can bother her. } 8... a5 { Level: nothing lost, but the queen stood better on c2. [%csl Bb3] }) (8. c5 { The c pawn pushes past, and his b pawn simply trades it away. } 8... b6 { About level: the pawn on c5 is gone next move, and the tension in the middle with it. [%cal Rb7b6] }) (8. Qa4 { The queen hunts on a4, but his knight comes to b6 and chases her off. } 8... Nb6 { Better for him: the queen must run, and his knight takes on c4 next. [%cal Rd7b6] }) (8. e3 { Just as good: the e pawn guards d4, though it shuts your dark bishop in and his bishop comes to a6 to hit your c pawn. [%cal Ge3d4] } 8... b6 { Level: a quieter game; the knight to d2 and the e pawn push was the plan. [%csl Be3] }) (8. Bg5 { The bishop comes out, but there is no pin: his bishop on e7 guards the knight, and he makes room for his own bishop. } 8... b6 { Level: nothing wrong, but the e pawn push is slower with the bishop out here. [%csl Bg5] }) (8. Bd2 { The bishop steps out of the way, but it does nothing on d2 and the e pawn plan is a move slower. } 8... b6 { Level: a slow set-up; he develops, and the trade on d5 opens the c column for both. [%csl Bd2] }) (8. Be3 { The bishop blocks your own e pawn, and the plan with the e pawn is gone. } 8... b6 { Level: he develops, and after the trade on d5 the c column is open for both sides. [%csl Be3] }) 8... b6 { He makes room for his light bishop with the b pawn; a rook to e8 first would change nothing, and taking on c4 would only hand the pawn to your knight. What now? [%csl Rb6] } 9. e4 { The e pawn strikes in the middle: now his d pawn must take, or be taken, and the middle opens either way. If he takes on c4 instead, your knight takes back. [%cal Ge4d5] } (9. Rd1 { Just as good: the rook first, the pawn strike next. } 9... Bb7 10. e4 dxc4 11. Nxc4 { Just as good: the rook first and the e pawn second; he took on c4 and your knight took back. [%csl Gd1] }) (9. b3 { Just as good, if slower: it guards c4, and his c pawn breaks out before your e pawn gets going. [%cal Gb3c4] } 9... c5 10. e4 dxe4 11. Nxe4 Bb7 { Level: the middle opens, but with his c pawn already on c5 he has nothing to fear. [%csl Rc5] }) (9. Re1 { Just as good: the rook waits for your e pawn push, and his c pawn breaks out first. } 9... c5 { Level: his c pawn hits d4 and the game opens on his terms. [%cal Rc5d4] }) (9. cxd5 { The trade opens the c column for his rooks as much as for yours. } 9... cxd5 10. Nb1 Ba6 { About level: his bishop is out, and his pawn on d5 walls off the long diagonal. [%csl Rd5] }) (9. b4 { The pawn push gives a pawn away for nothing: his bishop simply takes it. } 9... Bxb4 10. e4 h6 { About level: you got the pawn strike in, but a pawn down. [%csl Rb4] }) (9. c5 { The push locks the queen side, but it hands him the d pawn to aim at and a free b pawn trade. } 9... bxc5 10. dxc5 a5 { Better for him: your pawn on c5 is weak, his pieces have the middle, and the long diagonal is shut. [%csl Rc5,Rd5] }) (9. Ne5 { The knight jumps in, but he simply trades it off. } 9... Nxe5 { Level: your pawn takes the knight back, and your e pawn has still not moved. [%csl Re5] }) (9. e3 { The e pawn takes one step instead of two, and the middle stays shut. } 9... Bb7 { Level: solid, but the e pawn two squares was the plan, and his bishop settles on b7 in comfort. [%csl Be3] }) (9. Qd1 { The queen goes home, and his a pawn starts to run. } 9... a5 { Level: a move given back, and his queen side gains space. [%cal Ra7a5] }) (9. Nb3 { The knight steps away from the middle, and his bishop comes to a6 to hit c4. } 9... Ba6 { About level: the pawn on c4 needs a guard, and the knight on b3 is doing nothing. [%cal Ra6c4] }) 9... dxe4 { He takes on e4, the most popular choice, and his pawn now sits where yours stood. What now? } 10. Nxe4 { The knight takes back and stands proudly in the middle, hitting his knight on f6. The knight to e5 first was playable too. If his knight takes yours now, your queen takes back. [%cal Ge4f6] } (10. Ne5 { Also fine: the knight jumps in first, and the pawn is taken a move later. } 10... Bb7 11. Nxe4 Nxe5 12. dxe5 Nxe4 13. Bxe4 { About level: a lot of pieces came off, and your pawn on e5 holds his king side quiet. [%csl Ge5] }) (10. Ne1 { The knight steps back to e1: the pawn comes back, but his bishop gets to a6 first. } 10... Ba6 11. Nxe4 Nxe4 12. Bxe4 { Level: you took the pawn back, but his bishop on a6 hits your c pawn and your knight sits on e1. [%cal Ra6c4] }) (10. Ng5 { The knight jumps, but his pawn keeps running to e3 and breaks up your pawns. } 10... e3 11. fxe3 Bb7 { Level: your f pawn had to take, and your king's cover is split. [%csl Re3] }) (10. Nh4 { The knight goes to the rim, and his g pawn traps it there. } 10... g5 11. Nxe4 gxh4 { Better for him: your knight on the rim is gone, and all you have for it is his broken king side. [%csl Rh4] }) (10. Re1 { Look again: his pawn takes your knight on f3. [%cal Re4f3] }) (10. Qxe4 { Look again: his knight on f6 takes your queen on e4. [%cal Rf6e4] }) (10. Rd1 { Look again: his pawn on e4 takes your knight on f3. [%cal Re4f3] }) (10. b3 { Look again: the knight on f3 is hanging to his pawn, and this does nothing about it. [%cal Re4f3] }) (10. a4 { Look again: his pawn takes the knight on f3 while you push on the other wing. [%cal Re4f3] }) 10... Bb7 { His bishop comes to b7, behind his own c pawn; taking your knight with his own, on e4, was his better move. What now? } 11. Nxf6+ { Check! One pair of knights comes off, and his other knight leaves d7 to take back. The rook to d1 at once was just as good. [%csl Gf6] } (11. Rd1 { Also fine: the rook first, and he takes your knight himself. } 11... Nxe4 12. Qxe4 { About level: your queen on e4 eyes his pawn on c6; a fine game. [%cal Ge4c6] }) (11. Ne5 { The other knight jumps in, and after the trades your d pawn ends up on e5. } 11... Nxe5 12. dxe5 Nxe4 13. Bxe4 { About level: pieces have come off and your pawn on e5 cramps him, but the pressure on c6 is gone. [%csl Ge5] }) (11. Nc3 { The knight walks back to c3, and his c pawn breaks out at once. } 11... c5 { Level: his c pawn hits d4, and the knight has gone backwards for nothing. [%cal Rc5d4] }) (11. Bf4 { The bishop comes out, but his c pawn breaks out at once. } 11... c5 12. Nxf6+ Nxf6 { Level: the same trade, with his c pawn already breaking out. [%csl Rc5] }) (11. Qb3 { Look again: his knight takes your knight on e4, which only the queen was guarding. [%cal Rf6e4] }) (11. d5 { The pawn push throws the middle away: his pawns take, and the c column is his. } 11... cxd5 12. cxd5 Rc8 { Better for him: his rook owns the c column, and the pawn on d5 is about to fall. [%csl Rd5] }) (11. Nfd2 { The knight steps back to d2, a solid move, but the trade on f6 gives more. } 11... Qc7 { About level: his pieces have the squares they want, and your own knight on e4 blocks your bishop's long diagonal. [%csl Re4] }) (11. Be3 { The bishop comes out, and his c pawn breaks at once. } 11... c5 { Level: the middle opens before your knights are sorted out. [%cal Rc6c5] }) (11. Re1 { A rook to the e column, but his c pawn strikes first. } 11... c5 { Level: the rook had no target yet, and his c pawn is free. [%cal Rc6c5] }) (11. c5 { The c pawn pushes past, and his knight takes yours in the middle. } 11... Nxe4 { Level: your queen takes back on e4, and his b pawn takes your c pawn next. [%cal Rb6c5] }) 11... Nxf6 { He takes back with the knight, the natural recapture, and the right one. Nothing hangs. What is the most useful move now? [%csl Rf6] } 12. Rd1 { The rook stands behind your d pawn. Material is level, but his bishop on b7 is shut in behind his own c pawn, and your two centre pawns take all the room. Pushing the c pawn to c5 at once was just as good. [%csl Rc6][%cal Gc4c5] } (12. c5 { Just as good: the pawn push cramps him at once, and his knight has to find a square. } 12... Nd5 13. Bd2 bxc5 14. dxc5 { Slightly better for you: your pawn on c5 cramps his whole queen side, and his bishop on b7 is shut in behind his c pawn. [%csl Gc5] }) (12. Ne5 { The knight jumps in, but it leaves your d pawn to his queen. } 12... Qxd4 13. Bf4 Qd8 { Level: he ate your d pawn, and your pieces have some play for it. [%csl Rd4] }) (12. Qe2 { The queen steps to e2, a fine square, but she leaves the c column and his c pawn breaks out. } 12... c5 13. Rd1 cxd4 14. Nxd4 Qc8 { Level: the pawns in the middle came off, and the game is open and fair. [%csl Rc5] }) (12. Bf4 { The bishop comes out, but his c pawn breaks out before your rooks are placed. } 12... c5 13. Rad1 cxd4 { Level: the middle is open, and his bishop on b7 breathes again. [%csl Rc5] }) (12. Nd2 { The knight steps back and leaves the d pawn to his queen. } 12... Qxd4 13. Nf3 Qe4 { About level: a pawn down, and your knight went to d2 and back for nothing. [%csl Rd4] }) (12. d5 { The pawn push gives up the middle: his pawns take and his rook comes to the open column. } 12... cxd5 13. cxd5 Rc8 { Better for him: his rook owns the c column, your queen must move, and the pawn on d5 is loose. [%cal Rc8c2] }) (12. b3 { The b pawn guards your c pawn, but his c pawn strikes at once, before your rooks are ready. } 12... c5 { Level: his c pawn hits your d pawn, and his bishop on b7 comes to life. [%cal Rc5d4] }) (12. b4 { The b pawn grabs space but is loose; rather than take it with his bishop, he hits it with his a pawn so that his rook joins in on the a column. } 12... a5 { Level: your b pawn is hit, and his rook gets the a column. [%cal Ra5b4] }) 1-0


