{ The closed Catalan a moment after your e pawn struck in the middle: it stands on e4 and hits his pawn on d5. [%csl Ge4][%cal Ge4d5] }
9... Bb7 { He brings his bishop to b7 and leaves the pawns in the middle as they are, instead of taking on e4. The tension is yours to resolve. What now? [%csl Rb7] } 10. e5 { The pawn marches on and kicks his knight: its only good square is e8, on the back row, and a jump to the rim just loses time. [%cal Ge5f6] } (10. Rd1 { Also fine: the rook first, and he takes on e4 with pieces coming off. } 10... dxe4 11. Nxe4 Nxe4 12. Qxe4 { About level: a pair of knights came off, and your queen stands in the middle. [%csl Ge4] }) (10. cxd5 { Also fine: the trade first, and the pawn push comes next. } 10... cxd5 11. e5 Rc8 { About level: your pawn hits his knight, but his rook hits your queen first, so the knight has time to jump away. [%cal Rc8c2] }) (10. b3 { A slow move: he takes on e4 and his c pawn breaks out. } 10... dxe4 11. Nxe4 c5 { Level: the middle is open and his pieces are free. [%cal Rc5d4] }) (10. Ne5 { The knight jumps in, but he trades it off and your pawn lands on e5 without support. } 10... Nxe5 11. dxe5 Nd7 { Level: the pawn on e5 is a target now, not a weapon. [%csl Re5] }) (10. b4 { The b pawn is simply taken, and the push comes a pawn down. } 10... Bxb4 11. e5 Ne8 { About level: you got the pawn to e5, but you paid a pawn for it. [%csl Rb4] }) (10. Qd1 { The queen walks away from e4, and his knight takes the pawn. } 10... Nxe4 { Better for him: a free pawn in the middle, and his knight sits on e4 in your face. [%csl Re4] }) (10. exd5 { Taking on d5 is natural, but it frees his game instead of cramping it. } 10... cxd5 { About level: his c pawn took back, his bishop on b7 breathes, and your e pawn is gone. [%csl Rd5] }) (10. Re1 { The rook joins the e column, but his d pawn takes first. } 10... dxe4 { Level: he takes on e4 and the tension is gone; the pawn push was the move. [%csl Re4] }) (10. a3 { A quiet pawn move, and he takes on c4. } 10... dxc4 { Level: his pawn on c4 keeps your knight busy, and the e pawn never went to e5. [%csl Rc4] }) (10. Qd3 { The queen moves again, and he takes on c4 with a hit on her. } 10... dxc4 { About level: the queen has to take back, and his c pawn breaks free. [%cal Rd5c4] }) (10. c5 { The pawn pushes past, but his b pawn takes it and the middle comes apart. } 10... bxc5 11. e5 Ne8 12. dxc5 Bxc5 { Better for him: he is a pawn up, and his bishop on c5 aims at your king side. [%csl Rc5] }) 10... Ne8 { His knight goes back to e8, and his king side has lost its guard. Your pawn on e5 cramps him. What now? [%csl Re8] } 11. cxd5 { Your c pawn takes on d5: whichever pawn takes back, a column opens for your rooks, and his knight stays stuck on the back row. The rook to d1 first was fine too. [%csl Gd5] } (11. Rd1 { Also fine: the rook first, and the trade on d5 comes next. } 11... Nc7 12. cxd5 Nxd5 13. Qe4 c5 { About level: his knight found a fine square on d5 while you waited, and his c pawn is free. [%csl Rd5] }) (11. h4 { Just as good: the h pawn starts an attack before the middle is settled. } 11... Nc7 12. cxd5 Nxd5 { About level: the attack can wait; his knight reached d5 in the meantime. [%csl Rd5] }) (11. Re1 { The rook comes behind the e pawn, but his c pawn breaks out and the trades favour him. } 11... c5 12. dxc5 Nxc5 { Level: his knight came to c5 with a free hand, and the middle is his. [%csl Rc5] }) (11. b3 { A quiet move that lets his knight come to c7 and his pawns settle. } 11... Nc7 { Level: nothing wrong, but his knight on c7 now guards d5, and your edge has gone. [%cal Rc7d5] }) (11. Ng5 { Look again: his bishop takes the knight on g5. [%cal Re7g5] }) (11. c5 { The push gives a pawn away after the trades on c5. } 11... bxc5 12. dxc5 Bxc5 { Better for him: a pawn up, and his bishop stands well on c5. [%csl Rc5] }) (11. Qd3 { A queen move, when the trade on d5 was the move: it opens a column for your rooks. } 11... c5 { Level: his c pawn breaks and hits your d pawn, the base of your pawn on e5. [%cal Rc5d4] }) (11. a3 { A waiting move, and his c pawn breaks. } 11... c5 { Level: the middle opens on his terms. [%cal Rc6c5] }) (11. Nb3 { The knight goes to b3, and he takes on c4, hitting it. } 11... dxc4 { Level: his pawn on c4 hits your knight, and your queen takes it back; the knight trip won nothing. [%cal Rc4b3] }) (11. Qb3 { The queen goes to b3, and his c pawn breaks free. } 11... c5 { Level: a pawn of his can come to c4 and hit your queen, so she has to move again. [%cal Rc6c5] }) 11... cxd5 { He takes back with the c pawn and keeps his pawns together; taking with the e pawn was a touch better. What is the most useful move now? [%csl Rd5] } 12. Re1 { The rook stands behind your e pawn, which cramps his whole king side: his knight is shut in on e8, and your own knight has a road forward through f1. [%csl Ge5][%cal Gd2f1,Gf1e3] } (12. Nb1 { Just as good: the knight goes the long way round to c3. } 12... b5 13. h4 Rc8 { Slightly better for you: the knight is heading for c3 and his queen side is loose. [%cal Gb1c3] }) (12. Rd1 { Just as good: the rook behind the d pawn instead of the e pawn. } 12... Rc8 13. Qb3 Ba6 { Slightly better for you: a sound game, with his bishop finding a6 to annoy you. [%csl Bd1] }) (12. h4 { The h pawn starts an attack on the king side, where his knight is stuck. } 12... Qc7 13. Qb1 Rc8 14. b4 Qc2 { About level: a fine plan too, though he trades the queens off to cool it down. [%csl Gh4] }) (12. Qb3 { The queen steps to b3 and eyes d5, but that pawn is well guarded, and his a pawn starts to roll. [%cal Gb3d5] } 12... a5 13. h4 a4 { About level: his a pawn chased her for free. [%cal Ra5a4] }) (12. b4 { The b pawn is simply taken: a pawn for nothing. } 12... Bxb4 13. h4 Ba6 { About level: a pawn down, and his bishops are active. [%csl Rb4] }) (12. Ng5 { Look again: the knight on g5 hangs to his bishop. [%cal Re7g5] }) (12. a3 { A quiet pawn step: fine, but the rook belongs on e1. } 12... Rc8 { About level: his rook takes the open column and hits your queen, but she simply steps aside and your pieces still stand well. [%cal Rc8c2] }) (12. Qd3 { The queen steps up, but his bishop can hit her from a6, and your rook still stands in your knight's way. } 12... a5 { About level: his a pawn runs, and his bishop is ready to hit your queen from a6. [%cal Rb7a6] }) (12. Nb3 { The knight goes to b3, away from the king side where it is needed. } 12... a5 { About level: his a pawn chases the knight, and your king side plan is delayed. [%cal Ra7a5] }) (12. Nc4 { The knight jumps to c4, where his d pawn hits it at once. [%cal Rd5c4] } 12... Rc8 { Much better for him: his rook pins the knight to your queen, and his pawns take it next. [%cal Rc8c2] }) 1-0


