{ Your bishop has just reached the long diagonal, and his pawn on d5 is looking at your pawn on c4. [%cal Gg2b7] }
4... dxc4 { He takes your c pawn: that is the open Catalan. Do not chase the pawn yet; bring the king knight to f3 first. [%cal Yg1f3] } 5. Nf3 { The knight comes out and guards the d pawn; the pawn on c4 can wait, because he cannot hold it for long. If his bishop comes to e7 instead, castle and bring the queen out, as in the main line. [%cal Gf3d4] } (5. Qa4+ { Just as good: the check brings the pawn back at once. } 5... Nbd7 6. Qxc4 { Level: the pawn is back already, his knight stands on d7, and the knight to f3 comes next. [%csl Gc4] }) (5. Nc3 { Just as good: the knight comes out, though he can keep the pawn a while with a6 and b5. } 5... a6 6. Qa4+ Nbd7 { Level: the pawn comes back more slowly, and his knight on d7 blocks the check for free. [%csl Rc4] }) (5. e3 { The pawn guards d4 but shuts your dark bishop in. [%cal Ge3d4] } 5... c6 6. Nd2 b5 { About level: he holds the pawn with b5, and your bishop on c1 is boxed in. [%csl Rc1] }) (5. Qc2 { The queen hits c4, but your d pawn has no guard yet. } 5... Qxd4 6. Nd2 { Slightly better for him: his queen took the d pawn for free, and she sits in the middle. [%csl Rd4] }) (5. Bf4 { The London bishop, but d4 has only the queen to guard it while your knight stays at home. } 5... a6 6. Nd2 Qxd4 { About level: he grabs your d pawn, and the bishop on f4 has little to show for it. [%csl Rd4] }) 5... a6 { He guards b5 with the a pawn, meaning to prop his extra pawn up from there. A bishop to d7 instead is met by your knight jumping forward to attack it. Castle now. [%cal Ra6b5,Ye1g1] } 6. O-O { Your king is safe behind the bishop. His extra pawn on c4 is the whole story: the e pawn one step, the queen behind it, then the b pawn to break its guard. If he pushes his c pawn instead, trade it off; the pawn comes back anyway. [%csl Gg1] } (6. Ne5 { Just as good: the knight jumps in at once, and he answers with a check and a grab on d4. } 6... Bb4+ 7. Bd2 Qxd4 8. Bxb4 Qxe5 { Level: two pawns down for now, with both bishops out and his king still in the middle; a wild road that leads nowhere better. [%csl Gb4] }) (6. a4 { Just as good: the a pawn stops b5, and when his c pawn strikes, your knight goes after the pawn on c4. } 6... c5 7. Na3 cxd4 { Level: the pawn on c4 comes back by way of a3, but he has opened the middle first. [%csl Ra3] }) (6. Nc3 { Just as good: the knight on c3, though he can hold the pawn with b5. } 6... b5 7. Ne5 Ra7 { Level: his rook guards b7 from a7, the extra pawn stays his for now, and you must work for it. [%csl Rc4][%cal Ra7b7] }) (6. Bg5 { The bishop pins his knight to the queen, but b5 holds his pawn, and the pin leads nowhere. } 6... b5 7. Ne5 Ra7 { Level: a pawn down for now, with nothing to show for the pin. [%csl Bg5] }) (6. b3 { The b pawn hits c4 at once, but his check and the trade on b3 give you nothing. } 6... Bb4+ 7. Bd2 cxb3 8. Qxb3 { Level: he is still a pawn up for now, his check gained him time, and your queen is out early on b3. [%csl Bb3] }) (6. Qc2 { The queen hits c4, but his b pawn holds it and his knight lands on d5 in your face. } 6... b5 7. Ne5 Nd5 { About level: a pawn down, and his knight on d5 blocks your bishop's diagonal. [%csl Rd5] }) 6... Nc6 { His knight comes out and hits your d pawn. Guard it with the e pawn, one step only, so your bishop keeps its diagonal. [%cal Ye2e3] } 7. e3 { The e pawn guards your d pawn and opens the queen's road. If his rook goes to b8 now, to push the b pawn with a guard behind it, your knight drops back to d2 and collects his pawn itself. [%cal Ge3d4] } (7. Nc3 { Just as good: the knight comes out and e4 takes the middle; a sharper road. } 7... Rb8 8. e4 Be7 { Level: your pawns stand on d4 and e4, his extra pawn still lives, and both sides have chances. [%csl Gd4,Ge4] }) (7. a4 { Just as good: the a pawn runs to a5, so his b pawn can never come to b5. } 7... Rb8 8. a5 { Level: his queen side pawns are frozen on their squares, and c4 has no friends left. [%csl Ga5] }) (7. Qc2 { Just as good, and a gambit: the queen eyes c4 and lets him take on d4, and your knight comes out to win the pawn back with play. } 7... Nxd4 8. Nxd4 Qxd4 9. Nd2 { Level: two pawns down for the moment, but your knight hits c4, and his queen stands alone in the middle. [%cal Gd2c4] }) (7. Nbd2 { Just as good: the knight heads for c4, b5 holds the pawn, and a wild trade follows. } 7... b5 8. Ne5 Nxe5 9. Bxa8 Qxd4 { Level: you got a rook for the knight, he got two pawns for the exchange, and nobody is ahead. [%csl Ba8] }) (7. Qa4 { The pin looks tempting, but b5 kicks the queen home at once. } 7... b5 8. Qd1 { About level: she went to a4 and back, and his queen side pawns lean on each other. [%cal Rb5a4] }) (7. Ne5 { Just as good: the knight jumps in, and the trade on e5 takes the queens off. } 7... Nxe5 8. dxe5 Qxd1 9. Rxd1 Nd7 { Level: queens off, his pawn on c4 still there, and your pawn on e5 loose. [%csl Re5] }) 7... Bd7 { His bishop comes to d7 so that your queen can never pin the knight. Queen to e2: she eyes his extra pawn. [%cal Yd1e2,Ge2c4] } 8. Qe2 { The queen eyes his pawn on c4 from e2: one more attacker, and the pawn has only one defender to come. [%cal Ge2c4] } (8. a4 { Just as good: the a pawn fixes his queen side first. } 8... Rb8 9. a5 Bb4 { Level: his queen side is frozen and his bishop comes out to b4; the fight for c4 goes on. [%csl Ga5] }) (8. Nc3 { Also fine: the knight first, and the queen follows. } 8... Bd6 9. Qe2 O-O 10. Qxc4 { Level: the pawn is back a move later, with his bishop active on d6. [%csl Gc4] }) (8. Nbd2 { Just as good: the knight heads for c4 by way of d2, and his b pawn holds the pawn for now. } 8... b5 9. a3 { Level: his pawns on b5 and c4 lean on each other, and your knight waits. [%csl Rc4] }) (8. Qc2 { Just as good: the queen hits the pawn from c2; his b pawn holds it, and she goes to e2 anyway. } 8... b5 9. Qe2 Bd6 { Level: the plan is the same, a move later. [%csl Bc2] }) (8. Qa4 { The pin is an illusion now: his bishop on d7 shields the king, so the knight is free to jump. } 8... Nxd4 { Better for him: his knight took your d pawn and hits your knight, and his bishop now hits your queen. [%cal Rd4f3,Rd7a4] }) (8. Ne5 { The knight jumps in, but he trades and his knight lands on d5. } 8... Nxe5 9. dxe5 Nd5 { About level: your pawn on e5 is loose, and his knight sits in the middle. [%csl Re5] }) 8... b5 { He props the pawn up with his b pawn, and his queen side looks solid. If his bishop had gone to d6 instead, your queen takes on c4 at once. Now break the prop: the b pawn one step. [%cal Yb2b3] } 9. b3 { The b pawn hits his pawn on c4: if he takes, your a pawn takes back and the a column opens for your rook. [%cal Gb3c4] } (9. Rd1 { Just as good: the rook to the middle first, with e4 to follow. } 9... Bd6 10. e4 e5 { About level: a different plan, pawns in the middle instead of the b pawn break; his pawn on c4 lives on. [%csl Rc4] }) (9. h4 { Just as good: a king side poke first, and the b pawn break comes a move later. } 9... Bd6 10. b3 { Level: the same break, one move slower. [%csl Bh4] }) (9. Nc3 { The knight comes out, but his bishop hits it and the b pawn cannot break. } 9... Bb4 10. e4 { Level: his bishop hits your knight, and the b pawn cannot break while the knight stands on c3. [%cal Rb4c3] }) (9. e4 { The e pawn takes the middle, but his pawn on c4 still has its guard. } 9... Be7 10. Rd1 { Level: a fine centre, still a pawn down, and his pawn on c4 keeps its guard; the b pawn break was the way to hit it. [%csl Rc4] }) (9. a4 { The a pawn hits b5, but it is the b pawn that breaks the chain. } 9... Bd6 10. e4 { Level: his bishop comes out, and the pawn on c4 still stands. [%csl Rc4] }) (9. Ne5 { The knight jumps in, but he trades it off, and then a knight of his settles on d5. } 9... Nxe5 10. dxe5 Nd5 { About level: your pawn on e5 is far from its friends; taking his rook on a8 first was a touch better. [%csl Re5] }) 9... cxb3 { He takes, and that is his best: pushing on to c3 would just lose the pawn. Take back with the a pawn, and your rook looks down the a column. [%cal Ya2b3] } 10. axb3 { The a pawn takes back: he is still a pawn up, but your rook now eyes his a pawn down the half open column. Whether his bishop goes to d6 or e7 next changes nothing. [%cal Ga1a6] } (10. Ne5 { Just as good: the knight jumps in first, and your a pawn takes back on b3 a move later. } 10... Nd5 { He could grab your a pawn, but the trades that follow come out level; he hops to d5 instead. } 11. Nxd7 Qxd7 12. axb3 { Level: he is still a pawn up, as in the main line, with less pressure on him. [%csl Gb3] }) (10. Rd1 { The rook move lets his pawn run on to a2, and your rook has to take it there. } 10... bxa2 11. Rxa2 Bd6 { About level: he is two pawns up for now, and your rook on a2 is out of play, but his king is still in the middle. [%csl Ra2] }) (10. Nc3 { The knight comes out, but his pawn runs on to b4 and kicks it to the rim. } 10... b4 11. Na4 { Better for him: your knight sits on the rim, and his pawn on b3 lives on; he is two pawns up. [%csl Rb3] }) (10. Qc2 { Look again: his pawn on b3 takes your queen. [%cal Rb3c2] }) 10... Be7 { His bishop comes out quietly; a knight hop to d5 instead would only be kicked by your e pawn. Put your dark bishop on the other long diagonal, on b2 behind the b pawn. [%cal Yc1b2,Gb2d4] } 11. Bb2 { Your dark bishop sits on b2, behind your d pawn; both your bishops are out, and the rooks come next. [%csl Gb2] } (11. Rd1 { Just as good: the rook first, the bishop later. } 11... O-O 12. Nc3 Bd6 { About level: your rook is already in the middle, and the bishop still goes to b2. [%csl Gd1] }) (11. Nc3 { Just as good: the knight comes out, and his b pawn chases it to the rim. } 11... b4 12. Na4 Nb8 { Level: the knight sits on a4 for now, and his knight went home to b8 to guard everything. [%csl Ba4] }) (11. Bd2 { Just as good: the bishop comes out to d2, though it looks at little there. } 11... O-O 12. Nc3 { About level: on d2 the bishop is in the way of your knight and your rook. [%csl Rd2] }) (11. Ne5 { Just as good: the knight jumps in, and he trades and plants a knight on d5. } 11... Nxe5 12. dxe5 Nd5 { About level: your pawn on e5 is loose; taking his rook on a8 first was a touch better. [%csl Re5] }) 11... O-O { He castles. Now a rook to c1: the c column is half open, and his knight and his c pawn both stand on it. [%cal Yf1c1] } 12. Rc1 { Your rooks take the c and a columns: that is the open Catalan, where his extra pawn buys him only worries, and b5 is a target. [%csl Rb5][%cal Gc1c6,Ga1a6] } (12. Rd1 { Just as good: the rook to d1 instead, behind the d pawn. } 12... Nb4 13. Ne5 c6 { Level: his knight came to b4 and his pawn to c6, and your rook does its job there as well. [%csl Gd1] }) (12. Nc3 { Just as good: the knight first. } 12... Nb4 13. Ne5 { Level: his knight on b4 eyes your queen side, and your rooks still wait for their columns. [%csl Gc3] }) (12. h4 { Just as good: a king side poke, though it does nothing for your rooks. } 12... Be8 13. Ne5 { Level: a move on the edge, while your other rook still waits for the c column. [%csl Bh4] }) (12. Ne5 { The knight jumps in, but he trades it off. } 12... Nxe5 13. dxe5 Nd5 { About level: your pawn on e5 is far from its friends; grabbing his rook on a8 was just as good. [%csl Re5] }) (12. Nbd2 { Just as good: the knight develops to d2, and his knight jumps to b4. } 12... Nb4 { Level: his knight on b4 is annoying, and your rook has not found its column yet. [%csl Bd2] }) 1-0


