{ You have castled, and his a pawn stands on a6, ready to prop up his extra pawn on c4. }
6... b5 { He pushes the b pawn and means to keep his extra pawn for good. His pawns on b5 and c4 lean on each other. Is there a way at them? } 7. Ne5 { The knight leaps in and hits his extra pawn, and your bishop now sees his rook in the corner. If his rook climbs to a7 instead, the a pawn comes; if his c pawn steps to c6, the b pawn breaks at once. [%cal Ge5c4,Gg2a8] } (7. b3 { The b pawn hits c4 at once, but after the trade his rook guards everything from a7. } 7... cxb3 8. Ne5 Ra7 { Level: you can take back on b3 next, but his rook on a7 holds his queen side together, and your edge is gone. [%csl Ra7] }) (7. Ne1 { The knight steps back to open the bishop's diagonal, but it is slow. } 7... Ra7 8. a4 c6 { Level: his rook guards b7 from a7 and his pawns hold, with your knight on the back row. [%csl Re1][%cal Ra7b7] }) (7. a4 { The a pawn hits b5 first, but his bishop comes to b7 and the chain holds. } 7... Bb7 8. b3 cxb3 { About level: two pawns down for the moment, and his bishop on b7 guards everything. [%csl Rb7] }) (7. Nc3 { The knight comes out, but his b pawn kicks it and the chain holds. } 7... Bb7 8. a4 b4 { About level: his pawn on b4 chases your knight home, and the pawn on c4 stays his. [%cal Rb4c3] }) (7. e4 { The e pawn takes the middle, but his bishop comes to b7 to eye it, and his pawn chain holds. } 7... Bb7 8. d5 { About level: a pawn down, with his bishop staring at your centre. [%cal Rb7d5] }) (7. Qc2 { The queen hits c4, but his b pawn holds it and his bishop comes to b7. } 7... Bb7 8. b3 { About level: the pawn stays his for now, and your queen stands on c2 in front of nothing. [%csl Rc4] }) (7. Ng5 { The knight jumps the wrong way: his rook steps up to a7 and guards everything. } 7... Ra7 { Level: his rook on a7 holds everything, and your knight on g5 has no target. [%csl Ra7] }) (7. Bg5 { The bishop pins the knight, but his bishop comes to b7 and the pawn chain holds. } 7... Bb7 { About level: he keeps the pawn, and the trade on f6 gives you nothing. [%csl Rb7] }) (7. Nfd2 { The knight goes back to d2 to reach c4, but his c pawn props the chain up first. } 7... c6 { Level: his pawns on b5 and c6 hold the pawn, and your knight is slow. [%csl Rc6] }) (7. Bf4 { The bishop comes out, but it does not touch his pawn chain. } 7... Bb7 { About level: the extra pawn is his, his b pawn guards it, and your bishop has no target. [%csl Rb5] }) (7. e3 { The e pawn guards d4, but it is slow: his bishop comes to b7 and his pawns hold. [%cal Ge3d4] } 7... Bb7 { Slightly better for him: he keeps the extra pawn, and your knight never reached e5. [%csl Rc4] }) 7... Nd5 { His knight hops into the middle and blocks your bishop's view, while his two queen side pawns lean on each other. How do you shake the pair? } 8. a4 { The a pawn hits the back of his pawn pair: the pawn on b5 holds c4 and now needs holding itself. [%cal Ga4b5] } (8. e4 { Just as good: kick the knight first, then the a pawn; it comes to the same. } 8... Nf6 9. a4 Bb7 10. axb5 axb5 11. Rxa8 Bxa8 { About level: the very same position as the main line, reached by another order. [%csl Ga8] }) (8. b3 { Also fine: the b pawn breaks the chain first, and the a pawn follows. } 8... cxb3 9. a4 bxa4 10. Rxa4 Be7 11. Qxb3 { Level: he is still a pawn up, but his bishop sits on e7 and your pieces are free; a quieter road. [%csl Gb3] }) (8. Nc3 { The knight hits his knight, but after the trade his pawn lands on d5 and blocks your bishop. } 8... Bb7 9. Nxd5 exd5 10. e4 { Level: his pawn on d5 shuts the long diagonal, and your e pawn must fight to open it. [%csl Rd5] }) (8. e3 { The e pawn guards d4, but it shuts your bishop in and gives him time. [%cal Ge3d4] } 8... Be7 9. a4 Bb7 { Level: both his bishops are out, and his pawns still lean on each other. [%csl Rc1] }) (8. Qc2 { The queen hits c4, but his bishop comes to b7 and then his knight jumps in with a hit on her. } 8... Bb7 9. e4 Nb4 { Slightly better for him: his knight on b4 chases your queen, and his pawns hold. [%cal Rb4c2] }) (8. Nxc4 { Look again: his b pawn takes your knight. [%cal Rb5c4] }) (8. Bxd5 { Giving the Catalan bishop for a knight throws away your best piece. } 8... exd5 { Better for him: your bishop is gone, and his pawn on d5 shuts the game. [%csl Rd5] }) (8. Re1 { A rook move that waits, and his bishop comes to b7. } 8... Bb7 { Level: his bishop guards everything from b7, and the a pawn lever has not been played. [%csl Rb7] }) (8. Bd2 { The bishop steps out, and his bishop answers from b7. } 8... Bb7 { Level: the pawn chain holds for now, and the bishop on d2 does little. [%csl Rb7] }) (8. Nd2 { The knight goes back to d2 to reach c4, but it has only just arrived, and his bishop comes first. } 8... Bb7 { About level: two moves with the knight, and the chain on b5 and c4 still stands. [%csl Rc4] }) (8. Nxf7 { The knight grabs the f pawn, but his king simply takes it: a knight for a pawn. } 8... Kxf7 { Better for him: a whole knight gone for one pawn, and his king is safe enough. [%csl Rf7] }) (8. Bg5 { Look again: his queen takes the bishop on g5. [%cal Rd8g5] }) 8... Bb7 { His bishop comes to b7 to hold the knight and watch the diagonal. If his c pawn had come to c6 instead, your b pawn would break the chain at once. What now? } 9. e4 { The e pawn kicks the knight: it must leave d5, and your pawns take the middle. [%cal Ge4d5] } (9. b3 { Just as good: the b pawn breaks the chain first, and the a pawn takes on b5. } 9... cxb3 10. axb5 Bd6 11. Qxb3 O-O { About level: both pawns came back, and his bishops are active; a fine road too. [%csl Gb3] }) (9. axb5 { Just as good: the trade on b5 first, then the e pawn; it comes to the same. } 9... axb5 10. Rxa8 Bxa8 11. e4 Nf6 12. Nc3 { About level: the same position as the main line, by another road. [%csl Ga8] }) (9. Nc3 { The knight hits his knight, but the trade opens the long diagonal for his bishop, and your bishop goes. [%cal Rb7d5] } 9... Nxc3 10. bxc3 Bxg2 11. Kxg2 { About level: your Catalan bishop went for his, and he is still a pawn up. [%csl Rc4] }) (9. Qc2 { The queen hits c4, but his knight jumps to b4 and sends her home. } 9... Nb4 10. Qd1 { Slightly better for him: two queen moves for nothing, and his pawns hold. [%cal Rb4c2] }) (9. Bd2 { The bishop comes out, but his knight comes to d7 and trades off your best piece. } 9... Nd7 10. b3 Nxe5 11. dxe5 { Level: a pair of knights came off and the pawns come back on the queen side, but your grip is gone. [%csl Be5] }) (9. e3 { The e pawn guards d4, but it gives him time: his knight comes to d7 to trade off yours. [%cal Ge3d4] } 9... Nd7 { About level: your knight on e5 is about to go, and his queen side pawns still stand. [%csl Rc4] }) 9... Nf6 { The knight goes back to f6, and his bishop on b7 now stares at your e pawn. What now? } 10. axb5 { You take on b5: when his a pawn takes back, the a column opens for both rooks. [%csl Ra6] } (10. Nc3 { Just as good: the knight comes out, and his b pawn pushes past to keep the pawn a while. } 10... b4 11. Ne2 Nbd7 12. Nxc4 Bxe4 { Level: the pawn came back on c4, but his bishop took on e4 in return. [%csl Ge4] }) (10. d5 { The d pawn pushes, but his knight comes to d7 and trades yours off. } 10... Nbd7 11. Nxd7 Qxd7 12. Bg5 { Level: your knight has left e5, and the pawn on d5 is a target. [%csl Rd5] }) (10. Qe2 { The queen eyes c4, but your d pawn is loose: his queen takes it. } 10... Qxd4 { Better for him: two pawns up, and his queen hits your knight on e5. [%cal Rd4e5] }) (10. Nd2 { The knight heads for c4, but it leaves d4 to his queen. } 10... Qxd4 { Better for him: two pawns up, and your knight on e5 hangs to the queen as well. [%csl Rd4] }) (10. Bg5 { The pin first, but his bishop blocks it and the trade on b5 comes a move later. } 10... Be7 11. axb5 axb5 12. Rxa8 Bxa8 { Level: the pin went nowhere, and his bishop on e7 stands well. [%csl Bg5] }) (10. Bf4 { The bishop comes out, but the trade on a8 first is the point: his rook goes, and his bishop is dragged to the corner. } 10... Be7 { Level: he gets his bishop out and castles; the a column trade still comes, but with less bite. [%csl Bf4] }) (10. Be3 { The bishop comes out to guard d4, but the trade on a8 first is the point. [%cal Ge3d4] } 10... Be7 { Level: his bishop is out and he castles next, and the trade on the a column has lost its sting. [%csl Be3] }) (10. Qc2 { The queen hits c4, but she stops guarding d4, and his queen takes it. [%cal Rd8d4] } 10... Qxd4 { Better for him: he is two pawns up, and his queen in the middle hits your knight. [%cal Rd4e5] }) (10. Bd2 { The bishop comes out, but it cuts your queen off from d4, and his queen takes the pawn. } 10... Qxd4 { Better for him: two pawns up, and his queen sits in the middle of the board. [%csl Rd4] }) 10... axb5 { He takes back, and now the a column is open from end to end, both rooks standing on it. What now? } 11. Rxa8 { Rooks trade on the open column: your rook took first, so his bishop must take back in the corner. [%cal Ga1a8] } (11. Na3 { The knight develops, but it leaves the a column to him. } 11... Be7 12. Bg5 c6 { Slightly better for him: the knight stands on the rim, and the a column is his to use. [%csl Ra3] }) (11. Nxf7 { The knight grabs f7, but a knight for one pawn is not enough. } 11... Kxf7 12. Rxa8 Bxa8 13. d5 Bc5 { Better for him: his king took the knight and stands safe enough, and you have one pawn for a piece. [%csl Rf7] }) (11. Qe2 { Look again: his rook takes yours on a1, and nothing takes back. [%cal Ra8a1] }) (11. Nc3 { Look again: the rook on a1 is hanging, and his rook takes it. [%cal Ra8a1] }) 11... Bxa8 { His bishop takes back in the corner, and from there it looks all the way down the diagonal at your e pawn. What now? } 12. Nc3 { The knight guards your e pawn and hits his b pawn at once. A pawn down, yet his bishop is stuck in the corner, his knight still at home, and his b pawn can only be held by the c pawn, which shuts that bishop in. [%csl Ra8,Rb8][%cal Gc3b5,Gc3e4] } (12. b3 { The b pawn hits c4, but his knight comes out and the trades leave nothing. } 12... Nc6 13. Nxc6 Bxc6 14. bxc4 Bxe4 15. Bxe4 Nxe4 { Level: knights and bishops came off in pairs, and your grip went with them. }) (12. Be3 { The bishop comes out, but it leaves e4 to his bishop. } 12... Bxe4 13. Bxe4 Nxe4 14. Qf3 Nd6 { Level: he is two pawns up, but his king is stuck in the middle and your pieces fly; still, your grip is gone. [%csl Re8] }) (12. Qe2 { The queen guards e4, but the d pawn is left to his queen, and she takes it. [%cal Ge2e4] } 12... Qxd4 { Better for him: two pawns up, and she hits your knight from d4. [%cal Rd4e5] }) (12. Nd2 { The knight guards e4 from d2, but the d pawn is left to his queen. [%cal Gd2e4] } 12... Qxd4 { Much better for him: his queen took on d4 and now hits your knight, and your pieces are tangled. [%csl Rd4] }) (12. f3 { The f pawn guards e4, but his knight comes to d7 and trades off your best piece. [%cal Gf3e4] } 12... Nbd7 { Slightly better for him: your knight on e5 goes, and the f pawn has loosened your king. [%csl Rf3] }) (12. Bg5 { The pin first; his h pawn asks the bishop its plans. } 12... h6 13. Be3 { Level: the bishop reaches e3 a move later, but your e pawn still hangs to his bishop and knight. [%csl Re4] }) (12. Bd2 { The bishop develops, but now nothing guards your d pawn, and his queen takes it. } 12... Qxd4 { Much better for him: two pawns up, and his queen eyes your knight on e5 and your b pawn. [%cal Rd4e5] }) (12. Qc2 { The queen steps up, but she leaves your d pawn without a guard, and his queen takes it. } 12... Qxd4 { Much better for him: two pawns up, and his queen sits in the middle. [%csl Rd4] }) 1-0


