{ The open Catalan: his pawn has taken yours on c4, your knight is out on f3, and his king still sits in the middle. }
5... c5 { He hits your d pawn with the c pawn and still holds his extra pawn on c4. What first? } 6. O-O { Your king goes to safety first. Both pawns can wait: a capture on d4 would only open lines for your bishop on g2. [%csl Gg1] } (6. Qa4+ { Just as good: the check first and castling next; the moves meet the main line. } 6... Nc6 7. O-O Bd7 8. Qxc4 { Level: the very same position as the main line, by another move order. [%csl Gc4] }) (6. Nbd2 { Just as good: the knight heads for c4, though it leaves d4 with one guard less. } 6... cxd4 7. Nxc4 Bc5 { Level: you won c4 back but he took d4, so he is a pawn up for now, and your king is still at home. [%csl Bc5] }) (6. Nc3 { Just as good: the knight comes out, though your king stays in the middle while you win the pawn back. } 6... Nc6 7. Qa4 { Level: the queen pins his knight and still wins the pawn back, but your king has not castled. [%cal Ga4e8][%csl Re1] }) (6. dxc5 { Trading on c5 lets his queen come to d1 with check, and the queens come off before you castle. } 6... Qxd1+ 7. Kxd1 Bxc5 { Slightly better for him: your king stands on d1 and can never castle, while his pieces come out fast. [%csl Rd1] }) (6. Ne5 { The knight jumps in, but d4 is loose and his queen takes it. } 6... Qxd4 7. Qa4+ Nbd7 8. Nxc4 { Level: you got the c pawn back, but he took on d4, his queen sits in the middle, and your king is still at home. [%csl Bd4] }) (6. Qb3 { Look again: the pawn on c4 takes your queen. [%cal Rc4b3] }) (6. e3 { The e pawn props up d4, but he takes there and your queen takes back with your king still at home. [%cal Ge3d4] } 6... cxd4 { Level: the middle is open, and you have spent a move on a pawn that is gone. [%csl Rd4] }) (6. Qc2 { The queen comes to c2 to eye his extra pawn, and he takes on d4 first. } 6... cxd4 { About level: a second pawn gone for the moment, and your knight has to go and fetch c4. [%csl Rd4] }) (6. Be3 { The bishop guards d4, but his king knight can jump in and trade it off. [%cal Ge3d4] } 6... Nc6 { About level: his knight hits d4 and eyes the middle, and the bishop on e3 is a target. [%cal Rc6d4] }) (6. Bg5 { The bishop pins the knight, but it does nothing about his pawn on c4 or the pressure on d4. } 6... Nc6 { About level: his knight hits d4 while your king waits at home. [%cal Rc6d4] }) (6. Na3 { Just as good: the knight comes out to take on c4, and his knight comes to c6 first. } 6... Nc6 { Level: your knight takes on c4 next and he takes on d4, so you stay a pawn down for a while, with play for it. [%csl Ba3] }) (6. Bf4 { The bishop comes out, but his knight hops to d5 to hit it, and the pawn on c4 waits. } 6... Nd5 { Level: your bishop is hit, and the pawn on c4 is still his for now. [%csl Rc4] }) 6... Nc6 { His knight comes to c6 and adds a third attacker on d4. How do you get your pawn back? } 7. Qa4 { The queen pins his knight to the king and eyes c4 at the same time: his extra pawn cannot be held. [%cal Ga4c6,Ga4c4] } (7. Ne5 { Just as good: the knight jumps at his knight on c6, and the other knight goes round the rim to take on c4. } 7... Bd7 8. Na3 cxd4 9. Naxc4 { Level: the c pawn is back, but he took on d4 in return, and your knights stand well in the middle. [%csl Gc4] }) (7. dxc5 { Just as good: the queens come off, and his c pawn is then yours to collect. } 7... Qxd1 8. Rxd1 Bxc5 9. Nbd2 { Level: a quiet ending, and your knight is on its way to the pawn on c4. [%cal Gd2c4] }) (7. Nbd2 { Just as good: the knight heads for c4, though it blocks your queen's guard of d4, and that pawn falls for now. } 7... cxd4 8. Nxc4 Bc5 { Level: he is a pawn up for now, and his bishop is active on c5. [%csl Bc5] }) (7. Nc3 { The knight comes out, but his pawn takes on d4 and hits it, and its best square is back home. } 7... cxd4 8. Nb1 { Better for him: two moves wasted, and he is two pawns up. [%csl Rb1] }) (7. Qc2 { The queen eyes the pawn, but she is too late: he takes on d4 first, and your knight has to spend time getting c4 back. } 7... cxd4 8. Nbd2 Bd6 { About level: he keeps two extra pawns for now, and his pieces are out. [%csl Rd4] }) (7. Qb3 { Look again: the queen walks straight into his c pawn. [%cal Rc4b3] }) (7. Be3 { The bishop guards d4, but his knight jumps to d5 and hits it. [%cal Ge3d4] } 7... Nd5 { About level: the bishop is chased, and the pawn on c4 stays his. [%cal Rd5e3] }) (7. Bd2 { The bishop steps out, but it blocks your queen's guard of d4, and he takes the pawn. } 7... cxd4 { Slightly better for him: a second pawn gone for now, and the bishop on d2 still blocks your queen. [%csl Rd4] }) (7. Bg5 { The pin looks active, but he simply takes on d4. } 7... cxd4 { Slightly better for him: two pawns down for the moment, and the pin does nothing. [%csl Rd4] }) (7. e4 { The e pawn takes the middle, but nothing guards it, and he takes on d4 first. } 7... cxd4 { Slightly better for him: your pawn on e4 is loose and his pawn on d4 is a thorn. [%csl Rd4] }) (7. Na3 { Just as good: the knight goes for the pawn from the rim; he takes on d4, and your knight takes on c4. } 7... cxd4 { Level: you take on c4 next, but his pawn on d4 keeps him a pawn up for now, and your pieces have play for it. [%csl Rd4] }) (7. e3 { Just as good: the e pawn guards d4, he takes there anyway, and the trades leave the queens off. [%cal Ge3d4] } 7... cxd4 { Level: two pawns down for a moment, but the trades on d4 win one back and leave a level ending. [%csl Rc4] }) (7. Bf4 { Just as good: the bishop steps out, and his knight jumps to d5 at once to chase it. } 7... Nd5 { Level: your bishop is chased, and the pawn on c4 waits for a better moment. [%csl Rc4] }) 7... Bd7 { He breaks the pin, and now his bishop on d7 stands behind the knight, facing your queen. If he takes on d4 instead, the next chapter shows the way. What now? [%cal Rd7a4] } 8. Qxc4 { The pawn is back, the count is level, and your queen eyes his c pawn. If his b pawn comes to b5 she steps back to the d column; if he takes on d4, your knight takes back. [%csl Gc4,Rc5] } (8. dxc5 { Just as good: the trade on c5 first, and the queen takes on c4 next. } 8... Bxc5 9. Qxc4 { Level: the same pawn count as the main line, with his bishop already out on c5. [%csl Gc4] }) (8. Qd1 { The queen goes home and gives up on the pawn: he takes on d4, and his queen comes out to b6. } 8... cxd4 9. Nxd4 Qb6 { Slightly better for him: he is a pawn up, and his queen hits your knight and your b pawn. [%cal Rb6d4] }) (8. Bg5 { The pin looks active, but his c pawn takes on d4 first and your queen has to spend a move. } 8... cxd4 9. Qxc4 e5 { Slightly better for him: his pawns in the middle push your pieces around, and the bishop on g5 bites on nothing. [%csl Rd4,Re5] }) (8. Nbd2 { The knight heads for c4, but his b pawn kicks your queen first, and then d4 falls with a hit on the queen. } 8... b5 9. Qxb5 Nxd4 { Better for him: the knight hits your queen, the pawn is gone, and your queen on b5 is in trouble. [%cal Rd4b5] }) (8. Nc3 { The knight comes out, but he takes on d4 and your knight must go back. } 8... cxd4 9. Nb1 { Much better for him: two moves wasted and two pawns down. [%csl Rb1] }) (8. Ne5 { The knight jumps at his knight, but it leaves your queen alone: his knight takes, and then the bishop takes her. } 8... Nxe5 9. dxe5 Bxa4 10. exf6 Qxf6 { Lost for you: a queen for a knight. [%csl Ra4] }) (8. Bf4 { The bishop comes out, but his knight takes on d4 and uncovers his bishop on d7 against your queen. [%cal Rd7a4] } 8... Nxd4 { Slightly better for him: a second pawn gone, and your queen has to run back. [%csl Rd4] }) (8. Qc2 { The queen steps back, and he takes on d4 before she can take on c4. } 8... cxd4 { Better for him: two pawns down for now, and the pawn on d4 cramps your pieces. [%csl Rd4] }) (8. e3 { The e pawn guards d4, but his knight takes there anyway: it uncovers his bishop on d7 against your queen. [%cal Ge3d4,Rd7a4] } 8... Nxd4 { Better for him: the pawn is gone, your queen is hit, and when she runs his knight takes on f3 with check. [%cal Rd7a4] }) (8. Bd2 { The bishop steps out, and his knight grabs d4. } 8... Nxd4 { Better for him: a pawn for nothing, and you cannot take back: his bishop on d7 now hits your queen. [%cal Rd7a4] }) 1-0


