{ Your queen on a4 pins his knight to his king, and his king still sits in the middle. The pawn on c4 is yours to collect, unless he finds something first. }
7... cxd4 { He takes your d pawn with the c pawn and leaves his knight pinned; breaking the pin with his bishop first was better. What now? Look carefully. } 8. Nxd4 { The knight takes back. It looks loose on d4, but if his queen takes it, the pin on c6 wins the piece back. [%csl Gd4] } (8. Nbd2 { The knight comes out instead of taking back, and his pawn runs on to c3. } 8... c3 9. Nb3 Bd7 { About level: his pawn on c3 cramps your whole queen side, and he is two pawns up. [%csl Rc3] }) (8. Ne5 { The knight jumps at c6 instead of taking back, but he simply guards it and keeps the extra pawn. } 8... Bd7 9. Nxc6 bxc6 10. Qxc4 { About level: you got one pawn back, but his pawn on d4 still stands and his bishops are free. [%csl Rd4] }) (8. Qxc4 { The pawn first, but his pawn on d4 lives on and cramps your pieces. } 8... Be7 9. e3 e5 { Slightly better for him: his pawns on d4 and e5 own the middle, and your knights have no good squares. [%csl Rd4,Re5] }) (8. Rd1 { The rook eyes the pawn, but he guards it and keeps it. } 8... Bd7 9. Qxc4 e5 { Slightly better for him: the pawn on d4 is his to keep, and he has the middle. [%csl Rd4] }) (8. Nc3 { Look again: his pawn on d4 takes the knight. [%cal Rd4c3] }) (8. Be3 { Look again: his pawn on d4 takes the bishop, with a check to follow. [%cal Rd4e3] }) (8. e3 { The e pawn takes back a move later, but he takes on e3 first and the pawn count stays. } 8... dxe3 { Slightly better for him: your bishop has to take back on e3, and his pieces are the freer ones. [%csl Re3] }) (8. Bd2 { The bishop steps out, and his bishop blocks the pin from d7. } 8... Bd7 { Slightly better for him: the pin on c6 is broken, and the pawn on d4 is still his. [%cal Rd7a4] }) (8. Bg5 { The pin on f6 looks active, but his bishop comes to d7 and undoes your own pin. } 8... Bd7 { Slightly better for him: he keeps both pawns for now, and your bishop on g5 bites on nothing. [%cal Rd7a4] }) (8. Qc2 { The queen steps back, and his e pawn props up d4. } 8... e5 { Better for him: his pawns on d4 and e5 take the middle, and your knight is shut out. [%csl Rd4] }) 8... Qxd4 { He grabs your knight with the queen, and for now he is a piece up. It was his best try. Where is the catch? } 9. Bxc6+ { Check! The knight that stood pinned is gone, and his queen on d4 stands far from her friends. [%cal Gc6e8] } (9. Rd1 { The rook hits the queen, but she steps away and the knight on c6 is still guarded. } 9... Qb6 { Much better for him: a knight down for nothing, and his queen safe on b6. [%csl Rb6] }) (9. Nc3 { The knight comes out, but the piece count does not change: he is a knight up. } 9... Qb6 { Much better for him: a whole knight down, and his pieces are all safe. [%csl Rc6] }) (9. Be3 { The bishop hits the queen, but she goes home and the knight on c6 stays. } 9... Qd8 { Much better for him: a knight down, and nothing to show for it. [%csl Rd8] }) (9. Qxc4 { Look again: his queen on d4 guards that pawn and takes yours. [%cal Rd4c4] }) (9. Qb3 { Look again: his pawn on c4 eats your queen. [%cal Rc4b3] }) 9... Bd7 { He blocks the check with the bishop, and your bishop on c6 is attacked. What now? } 10. Rd1 { The rook hits his queen, and she cannot just step away. If his bishop takes yours on c6, your queen takes back with check. Had he blocked with the knight, this rook move wins his queen for a rook and a bishop. [%cal Gd1d4,Ga4c6] } (10. Be3 { The bishop hits his queen, but she grabs your b pawn and the bishops come off. } 10... Qxb2 11. Bxd7+ Nxd7 { Level: he grabbed your b pawn, and the fight goes on, but the rook move to d1 was sharper. [%csl Bb2] }) (10. Bxd7+ { The trade first gives his queen a safe home: your queen takes on c4, and nothing more. } 10... Qxd7 11. Qxc4 { Level: the pawn is back, but so is his queen, safe on d7. [%csl Bd7] }) (10. Bb5 { The bishop runs, and the queens come off on c4. } 10... a6 11. Qxc4 Qxc4 12. Bxc4 { Level: an equal ending, and the trick is over. [%csl Bc4] }) (10. e3 { The pawn hits his queen, but she steps to c5 and keeps everything guarded. } 10... Qc5 11. Bxd7+ Nxd7 { Better for him: the light bishops are traded, he keeps the pawn on c4, and his pieces are the better ones. [%csl Rc4] }) (10. Bxb7 { Look again: the bishop on d7 eats your queen on a4. [%cal Rd7a4] }) (10. Nc3 { Look again: his bishop takes yours on c6, and your queen cannot take back: his b pawn would take her. [%cal Rd7c6] }) (10. Bg5 { Look again: his bishop takes yours on c6, and if your queen takes back, his b pawn takes her. [%cal Rd7c6] }) 10... Bxc6 { He takes your bishop. If his queen takes your rook on d1 with check instead, your queen takes back and his bishop takes yours: a queen for a rook and a bishop. What now? } 11. Qxc6+ { Check, and the queen takes with gain of time: his queen on d4 still hangs to your rook. [%cal Gc6e8,Gd1d4] } (11. Rxd4 { Look again: his bishop takes your queen on a4 first. [%cal Rc6a4] }) (11. Nc3 { The knight guards your queen, but his queen steps to c5 and you are a piece down for nothing. } 11... Qc5 { Lost for you: a whole bishop and a pawn down, with nothing to show for them. [%csl Rc5] }) (11. Qxc4 { Look again: your queen no longer guards the rook on d1, so his queen takes it with checkmate. [%cal Rd4d1] }) (11. Qxa7 { Look again: his queen takes your rook on d1, and that is checkmate. [%cal Rd4d1] }) 11... bxc6 { He takes back with the b pawn. What now? } 12. Rxd4 { The rook takes his queen: the queens are gone, and his extra pawn is loose. A c pawn step to c5 or a bishop to e7 from him changes nothing. [%csl Rc4] } (12. Nc3 { The knight comes out, and his queen walks away: you are a whole queen down. } 12... Qb6 { Lost for you: his queen lives on, and yours is gone. [%csl Rb6] }) (12. Rf1 { The rook steps away from his queen, and she stays: you are a whole queen down. } 12... h5 { Lost for you: his queen on d4 is still on the board, and yours is not. [%csl Rd4] }) 12... Rd8 { He offers a rook trade on the d column, hoping to tidy up; a knight to d5 instead would not have saved the pawn either. Is the trade worth it? } 13. Rxc4 { The rook takes the pawn that stood on c4 instead, and his c pawn is next, with nothing guarding it. A rook trade would only have helped him. If he pushes it to c5 instead of checking, bring the knight out and play on. [%csl Rc6,Re8][%tail] } (13. Rxd8+ { The rook trade is tempting, but it tidies his king up and leaves his pawn alive. } 13... Kxd8 14. Nd2 c3 { Level: his c pawn runs forward and his king is safe; the pawn was there to be taken a move ago. [%csl Rc3] }) (13. Be3 { Developing is natural, but his knight jumps to d5 and the pawn on c4 stays his for now. } 13... Nd5 14. Bd2 { Level: nothing lost and nothing won, and his pawn on c4 still lives. [%csl Rc4] }) (13. Rd2 { The rook steps back and lets his knight into e4. } 13... Ne4 14. Rxd8+ Kxd8 { Level: a pair of rooks came off anyway, and his knight sits in the middle. [%csl Be4] }) (13. e3 { The pawn props up the rook, but he trades on d4 and keeps his extra pawn. } 13... Rxd4 14. exd4 { Slightly better for him: a pawn up, and his king can walk to safety. [%csl Rc4] }) (13. Bf4 { Look again: your rook on d4 hangs to his rook. [%cal Rd8d4] }) (13. Nc3 { Look again: his rook takes your rook on d4. [%cal Rd8d4] }) (13. Bg5 { Look again: the rook on d4 is hanging to his rook, pin or no pin. [%cal Rd8d4] }) 13... Rd1+ 14. Kg2 { The king steps up to g2, and his check is spent. Material is level, the queens are off, his king still stands in the middle, and your rook on c4 eyes his loose c pawn. [%csl Gg2,Rc6] } 1-0


