8. Qc2 Bxd2 9. Nbxd2 b5 { Level: nothing wrong, but the pawn on c4 is his for many moves yet. [%csl Rc4] }) (7. Qa4+ { Just as good: the check, though it helps his bishop out to d7 and the queen must move again. } 7... Bd7 8. Qc2 Bb5 { Level: his bishop sits on b5 guarding the pawn, and you spent an extra move. [%csl Bb5] }) (7. Bxb4 { Taking the bishop opens his a column, and the pawn that takes back on b4 is a nuisance. } 7... axb4 8. Ne5 O-O 9. Nxc4 { About level: the pawn is back, but his rook bears down the a column on your a pawn, and the pawn on b4 cramps you. [%csl Rb4] }) (7. Qb3 { Look again: his c pawn on c4 eats your queen. [%cal Rc4b3] }) (7. b3 { The b pawn hits c4, but his knight comes to c6, and the trade of bishops leaves his pawns strong. } 7... Nc6 { Level: he keeps the balance, and your b pawn did not win the pawn back. [%cal Rc6d4] }) (7. a4 { The a pawn stops b5, but his knight hits d4 at once. } 7... Nc6 { About level: his pieces come out with threats, and the pawn on c4 is still his. [%cal Rc6d4] }) (7. e4 { The e pawn takes the middle, and his knight simply takes it. } 7... Nxe4 { Better for him: a free pawn, and his knight sits in the middle of the board. [%csl Re4] }) (7. Ne5 { The knight jumps in, and his queen takes the d pawn it left behind. } 7... Qxd4 { Better for him: two pawns down for the moment, and his queen rules the middle. [%cal Rd8d4] }) (7. e3 { The e pawn guards d4, but it spends the move the queen wanted, and he develops and keeps the extra pawn a while. [%cal Ge3d4] } 7... Nbd7 { Level: a slower set-up; the queen to c2, eyeing c4, was the sharper way. [%csl Be3] }) 7... Bxd2+ { He trades the bishops, as expected. His knight to c6 instead lets your queen take the pawn at once, and his b pawn to b5 is met by the a pawn. Four of your pieces can take back. Which? } 8. Qxd2 { The queen takes back, and both your knights stay free: the one on f3 still guards d4. [%csl Gd2][%cal Gf3d4] } (8. Nbxd2 { Just as good: the knight takes back and heads for c4, though he can hold the pawn with b5 for a while. } 8... b5 9. O-O O-O 10. Rfd1 { Level: your knight wants c4, but his pawns hold it, and the game is long. [%csl Rc4] }) (8. Nfxd2 { The king knight takes back, and d4 loses its guard: his queen takes it. } 8... Qxd4 9. O-O Qc5 { Level: he has two extra pawns for now, and your knight on d2 is in the way. [%csl Rd4] }) (8. Kxd2 { The king takes back and walks out into the open, and it can no longer castle. } 8... b5 9. Nc3 Ba6 { Slightly better for him: your king is stuck in the middle, and his pawns hold c4. [%csl Rd2] }) (8. Kf1 { The king steps aside and leaves the bishop alone: it simply runs back, a whole piece up for him. } 8... Bb4 { Lost for you: a bishop for nothing, and your king cannot castle. [%csl Rf1] }) 8... c6 { He guards b5 with the c pawn: his idea is to hold his extra pawn with the b pawn next. What now? [%cal Gc6b5] } 9. a4 { The a pawn takes b5 from his pawns, so his pawn on c4 has no friend to lean on. If his b pawn pushes anyway, you take it and your queen jumps out to collect. [%csl Ga4,Rc4][%cal Gd2g5] } (9. Ne5 { Just as good: the knight jumps in; b5 comes anyway, and the game stays level. } 9... b5 10. b3 cxb3 11. axb3 { Level: you gave a pawn to get his back, so you are still one down, and his queen side pawns are the better ones. [%csl Bb3] }) (9. O-O { Just as good: castling lets him play b5, but the game stays level. } 9... b5 10. Ne5 Qb6 { Level: his pawns on b5 and c4 hold together, and your pieces must work hard for the pawn. [%csl Rc4] }) (9. b3 { The b pawn hits c4 at once, but after the trade his knight lands on d5 and you have gained nothing. } 9... cxb3 10. axb3 Nd5 { About level: still a pawn down after the trade on b3, and his knight sits in the middle. [%csl Bd5] }) (9. Nc3 { The knight comes out, but he develops too and the pawn on c4 stays his. } 9... Nbd7 10. b3 cxb3 11. axb3 { About level: a pawn still down after the trade on b3, with his pieces out and your b pawn weak. [%csl Rb3] }) (9. Qd3 { Look again: the queen steps straight into his c pawn's bite. [%cal Rc4d3] }) (9. e3 { A quiet pawn move, and his queen knight comes to d7 to guard his queen side. [%cal Rb8d7] } 9... Nbd7 { About level: nothing lost, but b5 is still open to him, and the pawn on c4 stays his for now. [%csl Rc4] }) (9. Qc2 { The queen moves again, and his b pawn comes to b5 at once. } 9... b5 { About level: his pawn on b5 guards the one on c4, and it is too late to stop his b pawn now. [%cal Rb7b5] }) (9. e4 { The e pawn takes the middle, but it is simply taken. } 9... Nxe4 { Better for him: a free pawn, and his knight in the middle hits your queen. [%csl Re4] }) (9. b4 { The b pawn tries to fix his queen side, but his b pawn answers and your pawns fall apart. } 9... b5 { Better for him: his pawn on b5 guards the one on c4, your b pawn is stuck, and the queen side is his. [%cal Rb7b5] }) 1-0


