{ The open Catalan once more: he still holds the pawn he took on c4, your knight is on f3, and nobody has castled. }
5... c6 { He plays the c pawn one step: it prepares b5 to hold the pawn, and takes d5 from your pieces too. Can you keep him busy before his b pawn moves? } 6. Ne5 { The knight jumps in and eyes both his c pawns, so the b pawn can no longer come to help. If he pushes to b5 at once, your a pawn hits it, and a knight to d7 lets your knight take the pawn. [%cal Ge5c4,Ge5c6] } (6. a4 { Just as good: the a pawn stops b5 before it starts, and his pawn on c4 cannot be held. } 6... Bb4+ 7. Bd2 a5 8. Qc1 { Level: the pawn on c4 cannot be held for long, and your pieces come out. [%csl Rc4] }) (6. O-O { Just as good: castling first, though he can play b5 and keep the pawn a while. } 6... b5 7. b3 cxb3 8. Qxb3 { Level: your b pawn went for his c pawn, so you are still one down, and his queen side pawns are rolling. [%csl Bb5] }) (6. Nc3 { The knight comes out, but b5 comes anyway and the knight has nothing to do on c3. } 6... b5 7. Ne5 a6 { Level: his pawns hold the extra pawn, and your knight on c3 is a target for b4. [%cal Rb5b4] }) (6. Qc2 { The queen eyes the pawn, but b5 holds it and his bishop comes to b7 against your bishop. } 6... b5 7. O-O Bb7 { Level: his queen side pawns hold the extra pawn, and the long diagonal is blocked. [%csl Bb7] }) (6. b3 { The b pawn hits c4 at once, but his check on b4 comes first. } 6... Bb4+ 7. Bd2 cxb3 8. Qxb3 { Level: still a pawn down after the trade on b3, with his bishop on b4 and your queen facing his pieces. [%csl Bb4] }) (6. Qb3 { Look again: the c pawn simply takes her. [%cal Rc4b3] }) (6. Bg5 { The pin on f6 is natural, but it lets him push b5 and hold the pawn in comfort. } 6... b5 { Level: his pawn on b5 guards the one on c4, and the pin changes nothing. [%cal Rb7b5] }) (6. Nbd2 { The knight heads for c4, but his b pawn guards the pawn first. } 6... b5 { Level: he holds the pawn for now, and the knight on d2 blocks your bishop. [%cal Rb7b5] }) (6. Bf4 { The bishop comes out, but it does nothing to stop b5. } 6... b5 { Level: nothing lost, but the knight jump to e5 hit his pawn chain before it was built. [%cal Rb7b5] }) (6. e4 { The e pawn goes two squares, and his knight eats it for free. } 6... Nxe4 { Slightly better for him: a pawn gone, and his knight sits in the middle. [%csl Re4] }) 6... Bb4+ { Check with the bishop. He wants to trade it or chase your king. A bishop to e7 or d6 instead is met by castling or by the knight taking the pawn. Block, or step aside? } 7. Bd2 { The bishop blocks and offers the trade: your knight stays on e5, and his bishop must decide. [%csl Gd2] } (7. Nc3 { The knight blocks, but his knight jumps to d5 and his pawns keep the extra pawn. } 7... Nd5 8. Bd2 b5 { Level: his pawns hold the pawn on c4, and your knight on c3 did nothing to stop it. [%csl Rc4] }) (7. Kf1 { The king steps aside and can no longer castle. } 7... O-O 8. Nxc4 Bc5 { Level: the pawn is back, but your king on f1 is in the way of your own rook. [%csl Rf1] }) (7. Nd2 { The knight blocks and leaves d4 with no guard: his queen takes it. } 7... Qxd4 8. Nf3 { Slightly better for him: he grabbed your d pawn, and your knight has to go back to f3: two moves wasted. [%csl Rd4] }) (7. Qd2 { Look again: his bishop on b4 eats your queen. [%cal Rb4d2] }) 7... Be7 { He pulls the bishop back: the check only cost him time. A grab on d4 with the queen instead is met by taking his bishop, and your pieces fly out while his queen is chased; a trade on d2 is taken back by the queen. What now? } 8. e3 { The e pawn props up d4, so your knight is free to go and take on c4 whenever it likes. [%cal Ge3d4,Ge5c4] } (8. Bc3 { Just as good: the bishop guards your d pawn from c3, and your knight takes on c4 next. [%cal Gc3d4] } 8... Nd5 9. Nxc4 a5 { Level: the pawn is back, and his knight on d5 hits your bishop. [%cal Rd5c3] }) (8. Bf4 { Just as good: the bishop comes out, and his knight hits it from d5 to trade it off. } 8... Nd5 9. Nxc4 Nxf4 10. gxf4 { Level: the pawn is back, but your king side pawns are split. [%csl Rf4] }) (8. Nxc4 { Taking the pawn at once leaves d4 hanging, and his queen takes it. } 8... Qxd4 9. Qc2 O-O { About level: pawn for pawn, and his queen stands in the middle of the board. [%csl Rd4] }) (8. O-O { Castling leaves d4 hanging, and his queen simply takes it. } 8... Qxd4 9. Bc3 Qxd1 10. Rxd1 { About level: the queens came off, and he keeps two extra pawns for now. [%csl Rc4] }) (8. Bxc6+ { The bishop takes on c6 with check, but his knight takes back, and after the knight trade you have given a bishop for one pawn. } 8... Nxc6 9. Nxc6 bxc6 { Lost for you: a bishop for a pawn, and his king is quite safe. [%csl Rc6] }) (8. Nf3 { The knight goes back to f3, and b5 comes with no punishment. } 8... b5 9. b3 cxb3 10. axb3 { Slightly better for him: you traded pawns on b3 and are still one down, his queen side pawns are rolling, and your knight went nowhere. [%csl Bb5] }) (8. Be3 { The bishop guards d4, but his knight jumps to d5 and trades it off. [%cal Ge3d4] } 8... Nd5 { About level: his knight trades itself for your bishop, and his pawn on c4 still has to be fetched. [%cal Rd5e3] }) (8. Qc2 { The queen eyes c4, but d4 is hanging, and his queen takes it. } 8... Qxd4 { About level: he took the d pawn, and your knight has to go and get his c pawn back. [%cal Rd8d4] }) (8. Nc3 { The knight comes out, and his queen takes the loose d pawn. } 8... Qxd4 { Better for him: a pawn for nothing, and his queen stands in the middle. [%cal Rd8d4] }) (8. a4 { The a pawn stops b5, but the d pawn was hanging, and his queen takes it. } 8... Qxd4 { Better for him: the queens come off and he is two pawns up. [%cal Rd8d4] }) 8... b5 { He throws the b pawn forward to hold his extra pawn, and his queen side is full of holes. If he castles instead, your knight simply takes the pawn, and his a pawn to a5 is met by yours to a4. Is there a way through? [%csl Rb5] } 9. Nxc6 { The knight grabs his c pawn: his knight must take back, and then your bishop takes it with check and runs through 