s pawns rule the middle. [%cal Re5e4] }) (9. Nb3 { The knight goes to the rim, and his e pawn comes to e4. } 9... e4 { Better for him: the knight on f3 is kicked, and the knight on b3 is doing nothing. [%cal Re5e4] }) (9. b3 { A quiet pawn move, and his e pawn pushes to e4 with a gain of space. } 9... e4 { Better for him: your knight is chased, and his pawn chain cramps you. [%cal Re5e4] }) 9... cxd5 { He takes back with the c pawn, and the c column is open. His pawn on e5 is hit twice and guarded twice. What now? } 10. Nxe5 { Your knight takes first, so that the trades on e5 leave his bishop out on that square, where your other knight can hit it. [%csl Ge5] } (10. dxe5 { Just as good: the pawn takes first, and the same position comes. } 10... Nxe5 11. Nxe5 Bxe5 12. Nf3 { Level: his bishop on e5 is hit by the knight, and his d pawn stands alone. [%cal Gf3e5] }) (10. e4 { Just as good, a wilder road: the middle opens and his pawns come forward. } 10... dxe4 11. Nxe4 Nxe4 12. Rxe4 { About level: your rook sits in the middle, but his f pawn is ready to chase it. [%csl Ge4] }) (10. Nb1 { The knight goes home, and his pawn comes to e4 with gain of time. } 10... e4 11. Nfd2 Nb6 { Slightly better for him: his pawn on e4 cramps your pieces, and his knight heads for c4. [%csl Re4] }) (10. Nh4 { The knight goes to the rim, and his e pawn pushes to e4 and cramps you. } 10... e4 11. f3 Re8 { Slightly better for him: the knight on h4 is out of play, and the e column belongs to him. [%csl Rh4] }) (10. Nf1 { The knight goes back, and his e pawn comes forward. } 10... e4 11. N3d2 Nb6 { Slightly better for him: both your knights have gone backwards, and his pawn on e4 rules. [%csl Re4] }) (10. Ng5 { The knight goes to g5 for nothing, and his e pawn pushes on. } 10... e4 { Better for him: his pawn on e4 cramps your pieces, and the knight on g5 has to come back. [%cal Re5e4] }) (10. Nb3 { The knight goes to the rim, and his e pawn comes forward. } 10... e4 { Better for him: the knight on f3 is chased, and his pawns take the middle. [%cal Re5e4] }) (10. e3 { The e pawn props up d4, and his e pawn pushes past it. } 10... e4 { Better for him: your knight has to run, and his pawn chain is a wall. [%cal Re5e4] }) (10. Qc2 { The queen moves, and his e pawn pushes to e4 with a hit on your knight. } 10... e4 { Better for him: the knight is kicked, and his pawns on d5 and e4 take the middle. [%cal Re5e4] }) 10... Nxe5 { He takes back with the knight. What now? } 11. dxe5 { Your pawn takes the knight, and only his bishop can take back. [%csl Ge5] } (11. e4 { The e pawn hits d5, but his knight on e5 has just taken yours and must be taken back. } 11... Nd3 { Much better for him: his knight forks your rook and the b pawn, and you are a knight down to start with. [%cal Rd3e1,Rd3b2] }) (11. a3 { A quiet move with his knight still standing on e5. } 11... Ned7 { Much better for him: his knight simply steps back, a whole piece up. [%csl Rd7] }) (11. Nb1 { The knight goes home with his knight still on e5. } 11... Nc4 { Much better for him: his knight stays, a whole piece up, and jumps to c4. [%csl Rc4] }) (11. Bxd5 { Look again: his knight on f6 takes your bishop on d5. [%cal Rf6d5] }) 11... Bxe5 { His bishop takes back and sits in the middle, hitting b2. What now? [%cal Re5b2] } 12. Nf3 { Your other knight hits his bishop, and his d pawn stands alone on d5 with no pawn to guard it: a target for your pieces. [%cal Gf3e5][%csl Rd5] } (12. Qb3 { Just as good: the queen hits d5 and b7, though his a pawn chases her. } 12... a5 13. Nf3 a4 { Level: the queen has to move again, and his a pawn has gained a free square. [%cal Ra4b3] }) (12. Rf1 { Just as good: the rook goes back the way it came, and the knight comes out next. } 12... Re8 13. Nf3 Bd6 { Level: his bishop retreats in good order, and the rook is back on f1. [%csl Bf1] }) (12. a4 { Just as good: a quiet pawn move, nothing lost. } 12... h6 13. Nf3 Bd6 { Level: the same picture a move later, with both wings quiet. [%csl Ba4] }) (12. Qc2 { Just as good: the queen eyes the c column, and his bishop simply steps back. } 12... Bc7 13. b4 a5 { Level: his a pawn hits your b pawn, and the bishop on c7 is safe. [%cal Ra5b4] }) (12. Nb3 { The knight goes to b3, where it hits nothing. } 12... Ne4 { Level: his knight lands in the middle, and your knight on b3 hits nothing. [%csl Be4] }) (12. e3 { A quiet pawn move, but it opens the road for his bishop to g4, where it hits your queen. } 12... Bg4 { Level: his bishop hits your queen, and the knight to f3 was the better move. [%cal Rg4d1] }) (12. Rb1 { The rook steps aside for nothing, and his bishop comes to f5. } 12... Bf5 { About level: his bishop takes a fine square, and the rook on b1 does little. [%csl Bb1] }) (12. Bxd5 { Look again: his queen takes the bishop on d5. [%cal Rd8d5] }) (12. b3 { Look again: his bishop on e5 takes your rook on a1. [%cal Re5a1] }) (12. e4 { The e pawn strikes at d5, but he takes, your knight takes back, and the queens come off. } 12... dxe4 { Level: after the trades on e4 and d1 the game is a dry ending. [%csl Re4] }) (12. Qa4 { The queen comes to a4 to hit nothing in particular; his bishop drops back to c7 and his knight heads for the middle. } 12... Bc7 { Level: a quiet game where your queen has wandered off. [%csl Ba4] }) 1-0


