{ Your g pawn has stepped up, and the Catalan bishop is one move from the long diagonal. Watch what he tries first. [%cal Gf1g2] }
3... Bb4+ { He checks on b4 before your bishop is out: this is the check on move three. Block with the bishop to d2 and let him decide what to do with his. [%cal Yc1d2] } 4. Bd2 { The bishop blocks and hits his. If his goes back to e7, he has spent a move; if it takes on d2, a pair of bishops comes off. Either way, your Catalan stands. [%cal Gd2b4] } (4. Nd2 { Just as good: the knight blocks, and his bishop stays on b4 a while. } 4... d5 5. Bg2 O-O 6. Nf3 { Level: a Catalan with his bishop still on b4, and nothing wrong with it. [%csl Gd2] }) (4. Nc3 { The knight blocks, but now the pin on c3 is real: that is the Nimzo Indian, another opening. } 4... O-O 5. Bg2 d5 6. Nf3 dxc4 { Level: he takes on c4 with a free hand, and your knight is pinned to the king. [%cal Rb4c3] }) (4. Qd2 { Look again: his bishop takes your queen on d2. [%cal Rb4d2] }) 4... Qe7 { His queen guards the bishop, so it can stay for now. A pawn to a5 or to c5 instead changes nothing: your bishop goes to the long diagonal all the same. Put it there now. [%cal Yf1g2,Gg2b7] } 5. Bg2 { Your bishop takes the long diagonal, as in every Catalan. If he castles now, the knight comes to f3 just the same; if he trades on d2, the queen takes back. [%cal Gg2b7] } (5. Nf3 { Just as good: the knight first. Here the other knight comes out too, and he trades on c3. } 5... Nc6 6. Nc3 Bxc3 7. Bxc3 { About level: he gave his bishop for your knight, so your bishop on c3 is the only one left on the dark squares. [%csl Gc3] }) (5. Nc3 { Just as good: the knight invites the trade on c3, and his knight lands on e4 with gain of time. } 5... Bxc3 6. Bxc3 Ne4 { Level: his knight hits your bishop, so it steps back or lets him trade it off. [%cal Re4c3] }) (5. e3 { Just as good: a quiet move, with the bishop still on f1. } 5... Bxd2+ 6. Qxd2 d5 { Level: he trades and takes the middle, and your bishop has not moved yet. [%csl Rf1] }) (5. a3 { Just as good: the a pawn asks the bishop to decide, and it trades itself. } 5... Bxd2+ 6. Nxd2 d6 { Level: the dark bishops are traded, and he builds his wall of pawns. [%csl Gd6] }) (5. Qc2 { Just as good: the queen comes out early, and his c pawn hits d4 at once. } 5... Bxd2+ 6. Nxd2 c5 { Level: his c pawn strikes your d pawn before you have castled. [%cal Rc5d4] }) 5... Nc6 { His knight comes out to help his e pawn reach e5 and lean on your d pawn. Bring the king knight out to f3. [%cal Yg1f3] } 6. Nf3 { The knight guards d4 and e5 both: his e pawn coming now would only be swapped off, and you would be the better for it. [%cal Gf3d4,Gf3e5] } (6. e3 { Just as good, and a little sharper: the e pawn guards d4, his e pawn comes, and your d pawn pushes past his knight. [%cal Ge3d4] } 6... e5 7. d5 Bxd2+ 8. Qxd2 Nb8 { Level: his knight went home, and your pawn on d5 takes his squares. [%csl Gd5] }) (6. a3 { Just as good: the a pawn asks the bishop, it trades, and his e pawn comes. } 6... Bxd2+ 7. Qxd2 e5 8. d5 Nd4 { Level: his knight jumps into d4, the square your d pawn has just left. [%csl Rd4] }) (6. e4 { Just as good: the e pawn goes two squares at once, and his pieces come into the middle. } 6... Bxd2+ 7. Qxd2 d5 8. e5 Ne4 { Level: his knight sits on e4 and hits your queen, and the middle is his. [%cal Re4d2] }) (6. Bc3 { Just as good: the bishop offers a trade, and his queen comes to b4 to hit your pawns. } 6... Bxc3+ 7. Nxc3 Qb4 { Level: his queen hits b2 and c4 at once, and your king is still at home. [%cal Rb4b2] }) (6. c5 { The c pawn runs ahead for nothing; he trades and undermines it. } 6... Bxd2+ 7. Qxd2 O-O 8. Nc3 d6 { Level: his d pawn hits your c pawn, and it was never going anywhere. [%cal Rd6c5] }) 6... Bxd2+ { He trades the bishop for yours, as he usually does here. Take back with the queen knight: it comes out and keeps the king knight where it is. [%cal Yb1d2] } 7. Nbxd2 { The queen knight takes back and is out at once, and your king knight keeps its post on f3. If he castles first, you castle too and the same position comes. [%csl Gd2] } (7. Qxd2 { Just as good by the numbers, but his knight jumps to e4 and his queen checks before you can castle. The knight recapture is calmer. } 7... Ne4 8. Qc2 Qb4+ 9. Kf1 { Level: your king has lost the right to castle, and the game is messier than it needs to be. [%csl Rf1] }) (7. Nfxd2 { The king knight leaves d4 unguarded, and his knight takes the pawn. } 7... Nxd4 { Slightly better for him: a pawn is gone, and his knight sits in the middle. [%csl Rd4] }) (7. Kxd2 { The king walks into checks and loses the c pawn. } 7... Qb4+ 8. Kc1 Qxc4+ { Better for him: a pawn down, and your king has nowhere safe to go. [%cal Rc4c1] }) (7. Kf1 { Look again: his bishop on d2 just took yours, and now it walks away a piece up. [%csl Rd2] }) 7... d6 { His d pawn steps one square, to guard e5 and let his bishop out. Castle. [%cal Gd6e5,Ye1g1] } 8. O-O { Your king is safe. Once your e pawn is on e4 and his comes to e5, push your d pawn past at once: otherwise it falls. [%csl Gg1] } (8. Nb1 { Just as good: the knight goes back to come out on c3, where it eyes d5. } 8... e5 9. Nc3 O-O 10. O-O { Level: the knight found its best square by the back road. [%csl Gc3] }) (8. e3 { Just as good: the e pawn one step guards d4, and the long diagonal stays open. [%cal Ge3d4] } 8... O-O 9. O-O e5 { Level: a solid set-up, and the fight for the middle goes on. [%csl Ge3] }) (8. a3 { Just as good: a small pawn move, and he answers with his own. } 8... a5 9. e3 e5 { Level: both a pawns have moved, and nothing has changed. [%csl Ba3] }) (8. e4 { Just as good: the e pawn two squares before castling, the same plan a move early. } 8... a5 9. O-O e5 10. d5 Nb8 { Level: his knight goes home, and your pawns take the middle. [%csl Gd5] }) (8. Nf1 { Just as good: the knight heads for e3 and d5, a slower dance before castling. } 8... O-O 9. Ne3 e5 { Level: the knight will reach d5, but he has castled and struck in the middle first. [%cal Ge3d5] }) 8... O-O { He castles; his a pawn to a5 instead changes nothing. Now the e pawn two squares, and the d pawn follows, to shut his knight out of the middle. [%cal Ye2e4,Gd4d5] } 9. e4 { Your e pawn takes the middle, and the d pawn is ready to push on and chase his knight. [%csl Ge4][%cal Gd4d5] } (9. Nb1 { Just as good: the knight comes round to c3 and d5 by the back road. } 9... e5 10. Nc3 h6 11. dxe5 dxe5 12. Nd5 { Level: your knight sits on d5, and his pawns are solid around it. [%csl Gd5] }) (9. a3 { Just as good: a small step on the wing, and the middle still waits. } 9... a5 10. Nb1 e5 { Level: his e pawn comes first, and your knight is still looking for a square. [%csl Ge5] }) (9. Qc2 { Just as good: the queen first, and his pawns come anyway. } 9... a5 10. e3 e5 { Level: a quiet game, with his pawns on a5 and e5 holding everything. [%csl Ge5] }) (9. b3 { Just as good: the b pawn guards c4, and the plan is the same a move later. [%cal Gb3c4] } 9... e5 10. d5 Nb8 11. e4 { Level: the pawn chain is built one move later, and his knight went home anyway. [%csl Gd5] }) (9. e3 { Just as good: one step only; his e pawn comes, and with no pawn of yours on e4 the push to d5 has less bite. } 9... e5 10. Qc2 a5 { Level: his knight stays on c6, and the middle is shared. [%csl Bc6] }) 9... e5 { His e pawn hits your d pawn, backed by the knight. Push past: the d pawn to d5 kicks that knight, and its best road is back home. [%cal Yd4d5] } 10. d5 { The pawn pushes past and hits the knight, and it goes back home. Now watch your pawns take the middle. [%cal Gd5c6][%tail] } (10. Nb3 { The knight steps aside, and his knight takes on e4. } 10... Nxe4 11. Nxe5 dxe5 12. Bxe4 Nxd4 { Level: after the trades you are a pawn down for a moment, but you win it back. Your edge is gone. [%csl Bd4] }) (10. Re1 { The rook guards e4, but his knight takes the d pawn for nothing. [%cal Ge1e4] } 10... Nxd4 11. Nxd4 exd4 { About level: a pawn is gone, and his pawn on d4 cramps you. [%csl Rd4] }) (10. Qc2 { The queen guards e4, and the d pawn falls to his knight. [%cal Gc2e4] } 10... Nxd4 11. Nxd4 exd4 { About level: a pawn down, and his pawn sits on d4 for good. [%csl Rd4] }) (10. c5 { The c pawn offers itself: he takes, you push past, and his knight jumps into the square your pawn left. } 10... dxc5 11. d5 Nd4 { About level: his knight sits on d4, and your c pawn is gone. [%csl Rd4] }) (10. dxe5 { The pawn takes, and his pawn takes back: the middle opens, and his knight keeps its square. } 10... dxe5 { Slightly better for him: the pawns are traded, his knight keeps c6, and your pawns no longer squeeze him. [%csl Bc6] }) 10... Nb8 11. b4 { Your pawns hold the middle, his knight went home to b8, and the b pawn grabs space on the wing. The check on move three is answered: block, trade, and push. [%csl Gd5,Rb8] } 1-0


