u and takes c3 from your knight. What now? [%csl Rd4] } 8. O-O { Your king is safe first. His d pawn cramps you for now, but it stands a long way from home. [%csl Gg1] } (8. e3 { Just as good: the e pawn hits d4 at once, and you castle a move later. } 8... Nc6 9. exd4 exd4 10. O-O { Level: the same picture, with the pawns traded a move early. [%csl Gd4] }) (8. a3 { Just as good: a quiet pawn move, and his knight comes out. } 8... Nc6 9. O-O a5 { Level: his a pawn stops your b pawn, and your a pawn did nothing. [%csl Ba3] }) (8. Bg5 { The bishop pins nothing much, and his knight simply comes out. } 8... Nc6 9. O-O Be7 { About level: his bishop asks yours what it is doing on g5. [%csl Rg5] }) (8. Qa4+ { The check only brings his bishop out, and your queen has to move again. } 8... Bd7 9. Qb3 Nc6 { About level: the queen went out for nothing, and he is a move ahead. [%csl Rb3] }) (8. c5 { The c pawn runs forward and falls. } 8... Qc7 9. O-O Bxc5 { Slightly better for him: a pawn is gone, and his bishop stands on c5 for free. [%csl Rc5] }) (8. Qb3 { The queen comes to b3, and his knight comes to c6 to guard the pawn. [%cal Rc6d4] } 8... Nc6 { Slightly better for him: his pawn on d4 is well guarded, and the queen is doing nothing on b3. [%cal Rc6d4] }) (8. Nbd2 { The knight on d2 blocks your bishop, and his queen comes to c7. } 8... Qc7 { Slightly better for him: your pieces are tangled, and his pawns on d4 and e5 take the middle. [%csl Rd4] }) (8. e4 { The e pawn goes two squares, and his knight settles on c6 behind a wall of pawns. } 8... Nc6 { Better for him: the middle is locked on his terms, and your bishop on g2 looks into your own knight and pawn. [%csl Rd4] }) (8. Nxe5 { Look again: his queen checks on a5 and takes the knight on e5 next. [%cal Rd8a5] }) (8. Nc3 { Look again: his pawn takes the knight on c3. [%cal Rd4c3] }) 8... Nc6 { His knight comes out and guards both his centre pawns. What now? } 9. e3 { The e pawn hits his d pawn: if his bishop comes to c5 to