 knight comes to d5 with a hit on nothing yet, but your queen on a8 is still boxed in. } 12... Nd5 { Better for him: his pieces close in on your queen while you make waiting moves. [%csl Ra8] }) (12. Bf4 { The bishop comes out, but he castles and his bishop to b7 traps your queen on a8. } 12... O-O { Lost for you: his bishop goes to b7 next, and then the queen on a8 has nowhere to go. [%cal Rc8b7] }) (12. Bd2 { The bishop steps out, and he castles: his bishop to b7 comes next, and your queen is caught. } 12... O-O { Lost for you: the queen on a8 is lost once his bishop comes to b7, and nothing can be done. [%cal Rc8b7] }) 12... Bxe3 { He trades the bishops, and the f pawn is the only piece that can take back. } 13. fxe3 { The f pawn takes back, and the f column opens for your rook. [%csl Gf1] } (13. Na3 { The knight goes to the rim with his bishop still sitting on e3. } 13... Bc5 { Much better for him: his bishop steps back, and the piece he just took is his for keeps. [%csl Rc5] }) (13. Nc3 { The knight comes out with his bishop still sitting on e3. } 13... Bd4 { Much better for him: his bishop sits on d4, and the bishop he took on e3 is his for keeps. [%csl Rd4] }) (13. Qb8 { The queen comes back with his bishop still sitting on e3. } 13... Bb6 { Lost for you: he keeps both bishops for your rook, and your queen is still stuck in his camp. [%csl Rb6] }) 13... O-O { He castles, and now his bishop and queen eye your queen in the corner: a trap is closing. What now? } 14. Nc3 { The knight comes out and covers d5 and b5, so your queen in his corner cannot be caught: a rook for a bishop, and your last minor piece is out. [%csl Ga8][%cal Gc3d5,Gc3b5] } (14. Rxf6 { The rook takes the knight, but the exchange goes straight back and the queen has to run. } 14... gxf6 15. Qf3 Bb7 { Level: the rook you won is given back, and his bishop on the long diagonal hits your queen. [%cal Rb7f3] }) (14. Qb8 { The queen steps back to b8, but his bishop steps aside and his rook hits her; she grabs a pawn, and his queen and bishop come to the middle. } 14... Bb7 15. Qxa7 Qd5 { About level: his queen and bishop rule the long diagonal, and yours is far from the fight. [%cal Rd5g2] }) (14. Qf3 { The queen runs home, but his bishop and queen line up against your king. } 14... Bb7 15. Qf4 Qc6 { Better for him: his queen and bishop aim at your king, and your knight is still at home. [%cal Rc6g2] }) (14. a3 { A pawn move while his knight finds d5. } 14... Nd5 15. Qb8 Nxe3 { Better for him: his knight took the e pawn, though his pawn to f6 first, boxing your queen in, was stronger still. [%csl Re3] }) (14. Nd2 { Look again: his queen on d7 takes the knight on d2. [%cal Rd7d2] }) (14. a4 { The a pawn runs, but his knight comes to d5 and your queen on a8 is still in a box. } 14... Nd5 { Better for him: the knight eyes your e pawn, and the queen on a8 has no road home yet. [%cal Rd5e3] }) (14. b4 { The b pawn runs, and his knight jumps to d5, hitting your e pawn. } 14... Nd5 { Much better for him: the pawn on e3 falls, and your queen is still shut in. [%cal Rd5e3] }) (14. Rc1 { The rook comes to the c column, and his bishop comes to b7: the queen on a8 is trapped. } 14... Bb7 { Lost for you: the queen can only grab a7, and then she has to give herself up for his bishop. [%cal Rb7a8] }) (14. Rd1 { Look again: his queen takes your rook on d1 with check, and it is mate soon after. [%cal Rd7d1] }) 1-0


