{ He grabbed your c pawn and holds it with b5, and your a pawn has just hit that pawn. His queen side is stretched thin. }
8... c6 { He props up his front pawn with the c pawn to keep his extra pawn. It looks solid, but it is the mistake: his pawn to b4 was the move. What now? [%csl Rc6] } 9. axb5 { The a pawn takes: it removes the pawn that his c pawn was guarding, and whatever takes back leaves a hole. A knight to d5 first does not help him: your queen simply takes the pawns back. [%csl Gb5] } (9. Bg5 { The bishop comes out, but the moment has passed: his pawns hold, and the trade gains nothing. } 9... Nd5 10. Bxe7 Qxe7 { Level: his knight sits on d5 and his queen side pawns hold together. [%csl Gd5] }) (9. Ne5 { The knight jumps first, and his knight blocks on d5; the a pawn should take first. } 9... Nd5 10. axb5 cxb5 11. Nc3 { About level: his knight on d5 blocks the long diagonal, and the rook in the corner is safe. [%csl Gd5] }) (9. Rd1 { The rook first lets him hold everything with a5. } 9... a5 10. Nc3 b4 { Level: his pawns on a5 and b4 hold the extra pawn, and the knight has to move. [%cal Rb4c3] }) (9. Nfd2 { The knight goes after c4 the slow way, and his pawns hold. } 9... Nd5 10. b3 cxb3 11. Nxb3 { About level: a pawn for a pawn, still one down, and the chance at his rook is gone. [%csl Gd5] }) (9. Nc3 { His b pawn kicks the knight, and his bishop comes to a6 behind the pawns. } 9... b4 10. Ne4 Ba6 { About level: his advanced pawns are safe, and his bishop guards them from a6. [%cal Ra6c4] }) (9. Qxc4 { Look again: his b pawn takes your queen. [%cal Rb5c4] }) (9. Nbd2 { The knight comes out, and his knight to a6 holds everything together. } 9... Na6 { About level: the moment to break his pawns has passed, and he keeps the extra pawn. [%csl Rc4] }) (9. Bf4 { The bishop comes out, but the a pawn was the move: his knight to a6 props his pawns up. } 9... Na6 { About level: his pawn chain holds, and your bishop has no target. [%csl Rc4] }) (9. e4 { The e pawn takes the middle, but his bishop comes to b7 and the chain holds. } 9... Bb7 { About level: the pawn on c4 is still his, and the long diagonal is blocked. [%csl Rb7] }) (9. Ng5 { The knight jumps to g5 a move too early: his h pawn kicks it, and nothing on a8 is loose yet. } 9... h6 { Slightly better for him: the knight goes back, and his pawns on the queen side hold. [%cal Rh7h6] }) 9... cxb5 { He takes back with the c pawn. His queen side is a wreck, and his queen side pieces are all at home. What now? [%csl Rb5,Rc4] } 10. Ng5 { The knight jumps aside and opens the long diagonal: your bishop hits his rook in the corner, and queen and knight both hit h7. He cannot save everything. Now watch. [%cal Gg2a8,Gc2h7][%tail] } (10. Nc3 { The knight comes out, but his queen guards everything and the chance is gone. } 10... Qb6 11. b3 cxb3 12. Qxb3 { Level: one pawn traded for another, you are still one down, and his rook in the corner is safe. [%csl Bb3] }) (10. Ne5 { The knight jumps to the wrong square: it opens the diagonal too, but his knight blocks it on d5. } 10... Nd5 11. Nc3 f6 { About level: his knight on d5 shuts the diagonal, and your knight on e5 has to run. [%csl Rd5] }) (10. Na3 { The knight goes to the rim, and he digs in. } 10... Bd7 11. b3 c3 { About level: his pawn on c3 is a thorn, and your knight on a3 is out of play. [%csl Rc3] }) (10. Nfd2 { The knight goes back, and his knight blocks the diagonal on d5. } 10... Nd5 11. b3 cxb3 12. Nxb3 { Slightly better for him: a pawn for a pawn, you are still one down, and his knight on d5 rules. [%csl Rd5] }) (10. Nh4 { The knight is on the rim, his knight blocks the diagonal, and your pawns get broken. } 10... Nd5 11. Nc3 Bxh4 12. gxh4 { Slightly better for him: your king side pawns are wrecked, and his knight sits on d5. [%csl Rh4] }) (10. Qxh7+ { Look again: his king takes the queen on h7. [%cal Rg8h7] }) (10. Bg5 { The bishop hits the knight, but his a pawn props the chain up and the rook on a8 is safe. } 10... a6 { Slightly better for him: the pawn chain stands, and your own knight on f3 still shuts the long diagonal. [%csl Ra6] }) (10. Rd1 { The rook moves, and his bishop comes to b7: the diagonal is closed. } 10... Bb7 { Better for him: his bishop guards the rook and shuts the long diagonal, and the knight jump has been missed. [%csl Rb7] }) (10. e4 { The e pawn takes the middle, but his bishop comes to b7 first. } 10... Bb7 { Better for him: the long diagonal is shut, and his extra pawn stays. [%csl Rb7] }) (10. Bf4 { The bishop comes out, and his bishop closes the diagonal from b7. } 10... Bb7 { Better for him: the rook on a8 is guarded, and your knight never got to g5. [%csl Rb7] }) (10. Rxa7 { Look again: his rook takes yours on a7. [%cal Ra8a7] }) 10... h6 11. Bxa8 hxg5 12. Bxg5 { Your bishop takes the pawn back: a rook for a knight, his queen side pawns scattered, and your bishop in his corner. That is what holding the pawn cost him. [%csl Ga8][%cal Gg5f6] } 1-0


