{ Your knight has just jumped to g5 and opened the long diagonal: your bishop hits his rook in the corner. [%cal Gg2a8] }
10... Nd5 { He blocks the diagonal with the knight and saves his rook. His pawn to h6, kicking your knight first, was the move. What now? } 11. Qxh7# { Mate. The queen takes on h7 with the knight guarding her, and his own rook and pawns box his king in. The knight that left f6 was the king's only guard. [%cal Gg5h7] } (11. Bxd5 { The bishop takes the knight, and it wins a rook too, but you have missed a mate in one. } 11... Bxg5 12. Bxa8 { Much better for you: a rook up, but the queen to h7 was mate on the spot. [%cal Rc2h7] }) (11. Rxa7 { The rook grabs a pawn and hits the rook, and it wins material, but the mate was there. } 11... Bxg5 12. Rxa8 { Much better for you: an exchange up, but his king could have been mated in one. [%cal Rc2h7] }) (11. Nxh7 { The knight takes the pawn, but it is the queen that mates on h7, not the knight. } 11... Re8 { About level: the knight has to run back, and his king is safe after all. [%csl Rh7] }) (11. Nf3 { The knight goes back home, and the whole attack is gone. } 11... Nc6 { Better for him: his pieces come out, and the mate on h7 is a memory. [%csl Rh7] }) 1-0


