{ The open Catalan: your e pawn has just stepped up to guard the d pawn, your bishop looks down the long diagonal, and his extra pawn still stands on c4. [%cal Ge3d4,Gg2b7][%csl Rc4] }
7... b5 { He pushes b5 to hold his extra pawn. It looks natural, but it is a mistake, and a big one: his bishop to d7 was the move. What now? } 8. Ne5 { The knight jumps in and hits his knight on c6: the moment that knight moves, the long diagonal opens all the way to his rook in the corner. If his bishop steps to b7 instead, take the knight twice and win a whole piece. [%cal Ge5c6,Gg2a8] } (8. b3 { The b pawn cracks his pawn chain, but it only chases one loose pawn; the knight jump wins a rook. } 8... Rb8 9. bxc4 bxc4 { Slightly better for you: he is still a pawn up, but the pawn on c4 is loose and your knight comes for it; nothing like the rook that was there for the taking. [%csl Rc4] }) (8. Nfd2 { The knight goes back to fetch the pawn, but his bishop comes out and the chain holds together. } 8... Bd7 { Level: the pawn chain holds, and the chance of a lifetime has passed. [%csl Rc4] }) (8. a4 { The a pawn pokes at b5, but his bishop comes to b7 and shuts the long diagonal. [%csl Rb7] } 8... Bb7 { Level: the diagonal is closed, and his pawns hold together. [%csl Rb7] }) (8. Qe2 { The queen eyes c4, but his bishop comes to b7 and guards the rook in the corner. } 8... Bb7 { Level: the long diagonal is shut, and the chance has passed. [%csl Rb7] }) (8. Nc3 { The knight comes out, but his bishop comes to b7 and the rook in the corner is safe. } 8... Bb7 { About level: he keeps the extra pawn, and the chance has passed. [%csl Rb7] }) 8... Nxe5 { He takes the knight, as nearly everyone does. What now? } 9. Bxa8 { The bishop eats the rook in the corner: a rook for a knight. His knight on e5 is still hit by your d pawn, and your bishop needs a door out. Now watch. [%csl Ga8][%tail] } (9. dxe5 { The pawn takes the knight back, and the rook in the corner is forgotten. } 9... Qxd1 10. Rxd1 Nd5 { About level: the queens are off, the rook in the corner lives, and he is still a pawn up; the big prize is gone. [%csl Ra8] }) (9. b3 { The b pawn cracks c4, but his rook steps out of the corner first. } 9... Rb8 { About level: the rook has stepped out of the corner to b8, and all you get is his knight back. [%csl Rb8] }) (9. Qe2 { The queen comes out, and his knight jumps to d5 and shuts the diagonal. } 9... Nd5 { Slightly better for him: the knight on d5 shuts the diagonal; your pawn takes his knight back, and he stays a pawn up. [%csl Rd5] }) 9... Nd3 10. b3 { Your b pawn hits his chain, and his knight on d3 has only that chain to lean on: a rook for a knight, and your bishop still sits in his corner. [%csl Ga8] } 1-0
